{"type":"text","timestamp":1794539477759,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"id":"prt_1d2e3597c001FHY2GBDbQopx22","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e34eac0019Sj3lYZDnnA5nB","type":"text","text":"I'll start by reading the tasks file and the notation block.","time":{"start":1794539477372,"end":1794539477759}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794539477770,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2e359c5001ohj4hS0sNCfAUi","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e34eac0019Sj3lYZDnnA5nB","type":"tool","id":"call_6763340565b645cdb5dd9543","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794539477445,"end":1794539477770}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794539477773,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2e3597f002sq17LAohX0PF1j","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e34eac0019Sj3lYZDnnA5nB","type":"tool","id":"call_6cf3732182d045d2a2514c14","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md, lines 1-239\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that generate one table of tracked commands, attach a typed command to the top recommendation, put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row, measure the typed block in the command capture and add the mirror visibility guard, hook health, a head length test and a brief cost script.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"typed command delivery tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a live model session, an arena or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `BLD` is `npm run build` in `ADV`, which must exit 0.\n31: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` its test `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `VCA` is `npx vitest run tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n32: - `DB` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`. `DBC` is `node $DB --check | jq -r .status`, which must print `fresh`. `CBR` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/README.md`.\n33: - `FU` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`. `FT` is `$ADV/tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` (new) and `VFT` is `npx vitest run tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `AR` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`. `ART` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` (new) and `VART` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n35: - `HT` is `$ADV/tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` (new) and `VHT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n36: - `CAP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`. `BASE` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-routing-baseline.json`. `CORPUS` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl`. `RS` is `npx vitest run tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md`.\n37: - `MBC` is `$ADV/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` (new). `OF` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs`. `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`.\n38: - `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`. `ST` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-status.ts`. `STT` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts` and `VST` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n39: - `SRM` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs`, `SRMT` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs` and `SRR` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/README.md`.\n40: - `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts`. `DSY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup.yaml`. `DCT` is `node --test $DS/tests/doctor-runtime-mirrors-contract.test.cjs $DS/tests/doctor-setup-orphan-line.test.cjs`. `IG` is `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n41: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print at least `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n42: - `EVAL` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`, which must print numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5.\n43: - `PY` is `python3 $ADV/tests/python/test_skill_advisor.py`, which must exit 0. `PAR` is `npx vitest run tests/parity/python-ts-parity.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed.\n44: \n45: Rules for every task:\n46: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it.\n47: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A script filters on `split` before it uses a prompt and prints row ids only.\n48: - No worker starts a live model session. Running the Claude prompt hook or the advisor CLI is not a model session, and both run in a sandboxed socket directory here.\n49: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T024 checks it.\n50: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, edits `AGENTS.md`, renames a command or edits a file under `.skilled/commands/create/`.\n51: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n52: \n53: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n54: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`), T002 (`$SP/typed-before.json`). Read-only for the repository.\n55: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that phases 005, 008 and 010 are committed, because they edit `AR`, `CA`, `ST`, `SCH`, `DSY`, `IG` and `RA`.\n56: - Wave 1: T003 (`DB`, `CA`), T004 (`SRM`, `SRMT`), T005 (`SCH`), T006 (`MBC`), T007 (`IG`).\n57: - Wave 2: T008 (`CA`, `CAT`), T009 (`ST`, `STT`), T010 (`DSY`), T011 (`SRR`).\n58: - Wave 3: T012 (`FU`, `FT`), T013 (`AR`, `ART`), T014 (`HT`).\n59: - Wave 4: T015 (`CAP`), T016 (`AR`).\n60: - Wave 5: T017 (`BASE`), T018 (`RA`), T019 (`CBR`).\n61: - Wave 6, one at a time because both start a sandboxed daemon: T020 (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`), T021 (`$SP/brief-cost.json`).\n62: - Verification, one at a time: T022, T023, T024, T025.\n63: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n68: ## Phase 1: Setup\n69: \n70: - [x] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3, .metrics.typed_reach_top3, .metrics.agreement, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached, .attachedMetrics.plain'`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `DBC`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VST`, `node --test $SRMT`, `node $SRM --check` and `DCT`, and write each command with its last output line or exit code into `before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` and a fourth no higher than 5, `TC` and `PY` exit 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `node $SRM --check` exits 0 and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 15. When a gate is red here, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because a red before number cannot be held.\n71: - [x] T002 Record each typed row's state (`$SP/typed-before.json`): run `node $CAP | jq '[.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key | endswith(\"-typed-01\")) | {id: .key, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1}] as $t | .attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [$t[] | . + {attached: ($a[.id] // null)}]' > $SP/typed-before.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 44, `jq '[.[] | select(.top1)] | length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 24 and `jq -r '.[] | keys[]' $SP/typed-before.json | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' '` prints `attached id top top1 `.\n72: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n77: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n78: \n79: ### Wave 1\n80: - [ ] T003 [P] Generate one table of every tracked command (`DB`, `CA`). In `CA`, above `// BEGIN GENERATED COMMAND ROWS`, add `export interface TrackedCommand { readonly policy: 'run' | 'start' | 'ask'; readonly ownerSkillId: string; readonly scorerRow: boolean; }` with a comment saying the table covers every tracked command, whether or not its bridge is active, so a typed command always has a policy and an owner. In `DB`'s `renderCommandRows(entries, policies)`, build a third map over every entry, with no enabled filter: key `entryRunnable(entry)`, value `{ policy: runnablePolicy(runnable, policies), ownerSkillId: entry.skillId, scorerRow: entry.runtime.typescript.enabled === true }`. Throw `[command-bridges] Conflicting tracked command <runnable>` when one runnable repeats with a different owner or policy. Render it after the `COMMAND_POLICIES` block as `export const TRACKED_COMMANDS: Readonly<Record<string, TrackedCommand>> = {` with one line per key in sorted order, shaped `  '<runnable>': { policy: '<policy>', ownerSkillId: '<owner>', scorerRow: <true|false> },`, and `};`. Run `node $DB`. Do not edit the generated block by hand. Verify: `DBC` prints `fresh`, `rg -c \"scorerRow: (true|false) \\},$\" $CA` prints at least 44, `rg -n \"'/create:goal': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'sk-doc', scorerRow: false \\},\" $CA` and `rg -n \"'/speckit:resume': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit', scorerRow: true \\},\" $CA` each print one line, `TC` exits 0, `npx vitest run tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- $ADV/scripts/skill_advisor.py $ADV/lib/scorer/projection.ts` prints nothing.\n81: - [ ] T004 [P] Add a command visibility count and a collapse guard to the mirror check (`SRM`, `SRMT`). Add `const COLLAPSE_ALLOWLIST = new Set(['create/skill.md'])` with a comment saying Claude Code reads a command folder holding `skill.md` as one skill and hides its siblings, and the existing case is kept until the commands are renamed. Add and export `commandVisibility(expectedLinks)`: it takes the `claude-commands` links whose path below `.claude/commands/` holds a folder, counts them as `total`, and counts Claude's `visible` as one per folder that holds `skill.md` plus every command in the other folders. Cursor's `visible` is `total`, because Cursor uses flat names. Add and export `collapseDrift(expectedLinks)`, which returns `COLLAPSE <folder>/skill.md hides <n> sibling commands on Claude Code` for each folder holding `skill.md` whose path is not in the allowlist. In `checkMirrors`, add those lines to `drift`, and before the PASS line print `[runtime-mirror-sync] visibility claude <visible>/<total>, cursor <total>/<total>`. Keep the PASS line as it is. In `SRMT`, add one case where synthetic links for `create/skill.md`, `create/readme.md` and `deep/review.md` give `{ claude: { visible: 2, total: 3 }, cursor: { visible: 3, total: 3 } }` and no drift, and one where an added `design/skill.md` beside `design/chart.md` gives `['COLLAPSE design/skill.md hides 1 sibling commands on Claude Code']`. Verify: `node --test $SRMT` reports `fail 0`, `node $SRM --check` prints a line containing `visibility claude 32/44, cursor 44/44` and exits 0, and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n82: - [ ] T005 [P] Add `hookHealth` to the status output schema (`SCH`). In `AdvisorStatusOutputSchema`, after `embeddingsHealth`, add `hookHealth: z.object({ invocations: z.number().int().positive(), rollingP95Ms: z.number().nonnegative(), rollingFailOpenRate: z.number().min(0).max(1), rollingCacheHitRate: z.number().min(0).max(1) }).strict().optional()`. Verify: `TC` exits 0, `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $SCH` prints 1 and `npx vitest run tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed.\n83: - [ ] T006 [P] Create `MBC`. `node $MBC [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` reads `CORPUS` under `--root` (default the current directory), keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, and with `--rows` keeps only the named ids, exiting 2 with `{\"error\":\"holdout_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for a row whose split is `holdout` and `{\"error\":\"unknown_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for an id it cannot find, before it spawns anything. It makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-cost-'))`, and for each row pipes `{\"prompt\":<prompt>,\"cwd\":<root>,\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cost-<id>\"}` into `node <root>/$HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to that directory and a 30,000 ms timeout. It takes `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` (or the empty string), and records `bytes` as its UTF-8 length and `estimatedTokens` as `Math.ceil(bytes / 4)`, the renderer's own four characters per token. It prints one JSON object `{ rows: [{ id, bytes, estimatedTokens }], totalBytes, medianBytes, maxBytes }` and never prints a prompt. At the end it sends `SIGTERM` only to a process whose command line names that directory and removes the directory. Verify: `node --check $MBC` exits 0, `node $MBC --rows cmd-speckit-save-typed-01 | jq -c '.rows[0] | [.id, (.bytes > 0), (.estimatedTokens == ((.bytes + 3) / 4 | floor))]'` prints `[\"cmd-speckit-save-typed-01\",true,true]`, `node $MBC --rows \"$(jq -r 'select(.split==\"holdout\") | .id' $CORPUS | head -1)\"` exits 2 and prints `holdout_row`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n84: - [ ] T007 [P] In `IG`, under `### Check the Install`, add one paragraph after the paragraph about Codex hook approval: to see the advisor answer a prompt, run `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the utils folder\"}' --format json | jq -r '.data.recommendations[0].skillId'`, which prints one skill id such as `sk-doc`, and when it prints nothing or `null`, run `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild`. Re-read the section first, because phase 008 also edits section 2. Verify: `rg -c \"advisor_recommend --json\" $IG` prints 1, and the documented command prints one line that is neither empty nor `null`.\n85: \n86: ### Wave 2\n87: - [ ] T008 [P] Add two typed command helpers (`CA`, `CAT`). `typedCommandIn(prompt)` lowercases the prompt and returns the longest `TRACKED_COMMANDS` key found at a position where the character before it is absent, whitespace, a backtick, a quote or `(`, and the character after it is absent or not a word character, colon or hyphen. It returns undefined when none is found. `typedCommandForTop(topSkillId, prompt)` takes that key and returns `{ command, policy, ownerSkillId }` from its row when `topSkillId` is the row's `ownerSkillId` or `commandRowFor(topSkillId)?.command` equals the key, and undefined otherwise. In `CAT`, add cases: `/create:skill-parent deploy-hub with three modes` gives `/create:skill-parent`, `/create:sk` gives undefined, `see docs/create:goal.md` gives undefined, `typedCommandForTop('system-spec-kit', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives `{ command: '/speckit:resume', policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit' }`, `typedCommandForTop('command-speckit-resume', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives the same and `typedCommandForTop('sk-doc', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives undefined. Verify: `VCA` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n88: - [ ] T009 [P] Report hook health in the status (`ST`, `STT`). Run after phase 008, which edits `readSemanticLaneHealth` and `handleAdvisorStatus` in the same file. In `readAdvisorStatus`, in the `output` object of the success path (the one inside `try`, right after `laneWeights: DEFAULT_SCORER_WEIGHTS,`), add `hookHealth` from `readAdvisorHookHealthSection(workspaceRoot)` (`$ADV/lib/metrics.ts`) when its `lastInvocations` is not empty: `invocations` is the length of `lastInvocations` and the three rolling fields copy over. Leave the error path as it is. Add a comment saying the p95 counts cache-hit invocations only. In `STT`, add one case that writes two valid diagnostic records with `persistAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord` for a temporary workspace root and reads `hookHealth.invocations` 2, and one case for a root with no records that reads no `hookHealth` key. Verify: `VST` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n89: - [ ] T010 [P] Show hook health under the setup advisor leg (`DSY`). Run after phase 008, which adds an embedder leg after this one. In the single list item that starts `- \"Leg advisor:`, append before its closing quote: ` When data.hookHealth is present, show hook p95 <rollingP95Ms> ms over <invocations> prompts and fail-open <rollingFailOpenRate as a percent> under the leg. It never changes the leg's status.` Change nothing else. Verify: `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $DSY` prints 1 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n90: - [ ] T011 [P] In `SRR` section 5, add two sentences after the check command: the check also prints how many tracked commands each runtime shows, and it fails when a command folder other than `create/` holds a `skill.md`, because Claude Code would then hide that folder's other commands. Verify: `rg -n \"skill.md\" $SRR` prints at least one line in section 5.\n91: \n92: ### Wave 3\n93: - [ ] T012 [P] Put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row (`FU`, `FT`). Import `typedCommandIn` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from `../command-attach.js`. Export `rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower)`: when `typedCommandIn(promptLower)` names a row with `scorerRow` false and that row's owner is at an index above 0 with `passes_threshold` true, return the list with the owner moved to index 0 and every other entry in its order. Otherwise return `ranked` unchanged. In `scoreAdvisorPrompt`, call `ranked = rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower);` right after the `applyDoctorHelperHandoff` statement and before `const passing`. Add a comment saying a typed command with no row of its own can only reach the reader through its owner, so the owner must lead. In `FT`, add cases on synthetic lists: `/create:goal x` with `sk-doc` passing at index 1 moves it first, the same with `sk-doc` not passing changes nothing, `/speckit:save x` (a command with a scorer row) changes nothing and a prompt with no slash changes nothing. Verify: `VFT` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n94: - [ ] T013 [P] Attach a typed command to the top recommendation (`AR`, `ART`). Import `typedCommandForTop` from `../lib/command-attach.js` and export `attachTypedCommand(output, prompt)`: when `output.recommendations[0]` exists and `typedCommandForTop(top.skillId, prompt)` returns a row, return `AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse` of the output with the first recommendation's `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId` set from that row, replacing any compiled route command. Otherwise return `output` itself. In `computeRecommendationOutput`, wrap both final returns, the cached one and the last one, as `return attachTypedCommand(<the existing expression>, input.prompt);`. Add a comment saying a typed command is the reader's own choice, so it outranks a routed mode. In `ART`, add cases: a top `system-spec-kit` with prompt `/speckit:resume cache work` carries `/speckit:resume`, `start` and `system-spec-kit`, a top `sk-doc` already carrying `/create:readme` with prompt `/create:goal retrofit` carries `/create:goal` and `start`, a top `mcp-tooling` with that prompt returns the same object and a prompt with no slash returns the same object. Verify: `VART` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n95: - [ ] T014 [P] Create `HT`. For every key of `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, render `renderAdvisorBrief` with one passing `skill` recommendation for the row's owner that carries the key as `command`, its `policy` and its `ownerSkillId`, freshness `live` and `metrics: { tokenCap: 80 }`, in the shape `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` uses. Do the same for every id in `COMMAND_ROWS` as a `command` recommendation. Take the head before `DIRECTIVES_LABEL` and assert that `Buffer.byteLength(head, 'utf8')` is at most `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT`, that the head does not end with `...` and that it contains `<command> (<policy>)`. Define `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT` as `80 * 4` with a comment naming the renderer's default token cap and its four characters per token. Add one case where a synthetic 330-character command fails the same check. Verify: `VHT` reports 0 failed and at least 45 passed tests.\n96: \n97: ### Wave 4\n98: - [ ] T015 Add the typed block to the command capture (`CAP`). Import `typedCommandForTop` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from the built attach module the script already loads. For each row with `row_type` `typed`, compute `delivered` as `typedCommandForTop(topId, row.prompt)?.command ?? attached`, the same precedence as the handler, and `policy` as `TRACKED_COMMANDS[delivered]?.policy ?? null`. Count `own` when `top1Hit`, else `attached` when `delivered` equals the expected command and `policy` is not null, in a tally built with `emptyAttachedTally` and `addAttachedRow`. Add `typed: attachedTallyJson(...)` to `attachedMetrics`, and `typedRows`, keyed by row id in sorted order, each `{ expected_command, top, carried, command, policy }`, where `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null. Write no prompt text. Leave every existing key and its computation unchanged. Verify: after `BLD`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed'` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `node $CAP | jq -c '[.attachedMetrics.typedRows[] | keys] | unique'` prints `[[\"carried\",\"command\",\"expected_command\",\"policy\",\"top\"]]` and `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n99: - [ ] T016 Take a hub-attached command's policy from the new table (`AR`). In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace the spread that reads `COMMAND_POLICIES` with `...(Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command) ? { policy: TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy } : {})`, import `TRACKED_COMMANDS` and drop `COMMAND_POLICIES` from the import when nothing else uses it. Verify: `rg -c \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" $AR` prints nothing or 0, `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS\\[command\\]\" $AR` prints 1, `TC` exits 0 and `VART` reports 0 failed.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 5\n102: - [ ] T017 Rebuild and recapture the command baseline (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}`, `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `jq '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3' $BASE` prints at least the value in `$SP/before.txt`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain' $BASE` equals the line in `$SP/before.txt` and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n103: - [ ] T018 [P] In `RA`, describe `attachedMetrics.typed` and `attachedMetrics.typedRows`, the rule that a typed command travels on its owner, and `measure-brief-cost.mjs` with its flags, its output keys and its sandbox. Verify: `rg -c \"attachedMetrics.typed|typedRows|measure-brief-cost\" $RA` prints at least 3.\n104: - [ ] T019 [P] In `CBR`, describe `TRACKED_COMMANDS`: one row per bridge entry with its policy, its owner and `scorerRow`, generated whether or not the bridge is active. Verify: `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS|scorerRow\" $CBR` prints at least 2.\n105: \n106: ### Wave 6\n107: - [ ] T020 Check the real heads (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`). Write a script that reads `CORPUS`, keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-typed-'))`, pipes each row's payload (the shape of T006, session id `typed-<id>`) into `node $HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to it, takes the first line of `additionalContext` and reads it with `parseOffer` from `OF`. It writes `[{ id, offeredCommand, offeredPolicy, commandMatches, policyPresent, labelAgrees }]` to `typed-heads.json`, where `commandMatches` compares the `/<namespace>:<name>` part of the offer with the row's `expected_command`, `policyPresent` is true for `run`, `start` or `ask` and `labelAgrees` compares the policy with the row's `expected_policy`. It stops and removes its sandbox as T006 does and prints no prompt. Verify: `jq '[.[] | select(.commandMatches and .policyPresent)] | length' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints 44, `jq -r '[.[] | select(.labelAgrees | not) | .id] | join(\",\")' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints `cmd-design-extract-typed-01,cmd-doctor-env-typed-01,cmd-doctor-update-typed-01` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-typed-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n108: - [ ] T021 Measure the brief cost (`$SP/brief-cost.json`). Run `node $MBC > $SP/brief-cost.json`. Verify: `jq '.rows | length' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints 44, `jq '.maxBytes > 0 and .medianBytes > 0' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints `true` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n109: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n114: ## Phase 3: Verification\n115: \n116: - [ ] T022 Run every floor. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt` and no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5, `TC` exits 0, `PY` exits 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VFT`, `VART`, `VHT` and `VST` report 0 failed, `node --test $SRMT` and `DCT` report `fail 0` and `node $SRM --check` exits 0. Write the outputs beside the before lines in `$SP/after.txt`.\n117: - [ ] T023 Read hook health live. Make one directory with `D=$(mktemp -d \"$TMPDIR/ssa-status-XXXXXX\")`, run `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR=\"$D\" node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --no-warm-only | jq -r '.data.hookHealth.rollingP95Ms | type'`, then stop only a process whose command line names `$D` and remove `$D`. The Claude prompt hook runs in T020 and T021 wrote the diagnostic records it reads. Verify: the command prints `number`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-status-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n118: - [ ] T024 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled INSTALL-SKILLED.md AGENTS.md` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n119: - [ ] T025 Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n120: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n125: ## Completion Criteria\n126: \n127: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n128: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n129: - [ ] The committed baseline reads `attachedMetrics.typed.combined` 44 of 44 and every floor holds against `scratch/before.txt`\n130: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n131: \n132: ---\n133: \n134: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n135: ## Cross-References\n136: \n137: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n138: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n139: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n140: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research.md`\n141: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## Verification Checklist\n146: \n147: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n148: ## Verification Protocol\n149: \n150: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n151: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n152: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n153: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n154: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n155: \n156: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n157: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n162: ## Pre-Implementation\n163: \n164: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the typed row state are in `scratch/` (T001, T002).\n165: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The coordinator confirmed phases 005, 008 and 010 are committed before wave 1 started.\n166: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n167: \n168: ---\n169: \n170: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n171: ## Code Quality\n172: \n173: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `npm run typecheck` in `ADV` exits 0.\n174: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id (T024).\n175: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The generated block is written by the deriver only, and its check prints `fresh` (T003, T022).\n176: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] A prompt with no typed command leaves the recommendation unchanged (T012, T013).\n177: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n178: \n179: ---\n180: \n181: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n182: ## Testing Checklist\n183: \n184: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds (T022).\n185: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] The baseline reads 44 of 44 typed and the slice test passes (T017).\n186: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] The parity test and the Python test pass (T022).\n187: - [ ] CHK-023 [P1] The real heads of all 44 typed rows carry their command and a policy (T020).\n188: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n189: \n190: ---\n191: \n192: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n193: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n194: \n195: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] The gap is class `delivery`: the scorer finds the owner, and the command is lost between the scorer and the head.\n196: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Every reader of the policy table is listed: `rg -n \"COMMAND_POLICIES|TRACKED_COMMANDS\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'`.\n197: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P1] Both return paths of the recommend handler apply the typed step (T013).\n198: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on.\n199: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n204: ## Security\n205: \n206: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task reads or prints holdout prompt text, and the cost script refuses a holdout row (T006).\n207: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] No task writes to the home folder or installs anything (T024).\n208: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n213: ## Documentation\n214: \n215: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same numbers.\n216: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The install guide, the setup asset and the three READMEs match the code (T007, T010, T011, T018, T019).\n217: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n222: ## File Organization\n223: \n224: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Probes, measurements and the hook check live in `scratch/` only.\n225: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n230: ## Verification Summary\n231: \n232: | Category | Total | Verified |\n233: |----------|-------|----------|\n234: | P0 Items | 12 | 0/12 |\n235: | P1 Items | 7 | 0/7 |\n236: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n237: \n238: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n239: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md, lines 1-239\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that generate one table of tracked commands, attach a typed command to the top recommendation, put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row, measure the typed block in the command capture and add the mirror visibility guard, hook health, a head length test and a brief cost script.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"typed command delivery tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 11: typed-command-delivery-and-guards\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a live model session, an arena or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `BLD` is `npm run build` in `ADV`, which must exit 0.\n31: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` its test `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `VCA` is `npx vitest run tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n32: - `DB` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`. `DBC` is `node $DB --check | jq -r .status`, which must print `fresh`. `CBR` is `$ADV/scripts/command-bridges/README.md`.\n33: - `FU` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`. `FT` is `$ADV/tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` (new) and `VFT` is `npx vitest run tests/scorer/typed-command-owner.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `AR` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`. `ART` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` (new) and `VART` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-typed-command.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n35: - `HT` is `$ADV/tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` (new) and `VHT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n36: - `CAP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`. `BASE` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-routing-baseline.json`. `CORPUS` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl`. `RS` is `npx vitest run tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md`.\n37: - `MBC` is `$ADV/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` (new). `OF` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs`. `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`.\n38: - `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`. `ST` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-status.ts`. `STT` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts` and `VST` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n39: - `SRM` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs`, `SRMT` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs` and `SRR` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/README.md`.\n40: - `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts`. `DSY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup.yaml`. `DCT` is `node --test $DS/tests/doctor-runtime-mirrors-contract.test.cjs $DS/tests/doctor-setup-orphan-line.test.cjs`. `IG` is `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n41: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print at least `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n42: - `EVAL` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`, which must print numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5.\n43: - `PY` is `python3 $ADV/tests/python/test_skill_advisor.py`, which must exit 0. `PAR` is `npx vitest run tests/parity/python-ts-parity.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed.\n44: \n45: Rules for every task:\n46: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it.\n47: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A script filters on `split` before it uses a prompt and prints row ids only.\n48: - No worker starts a live model session. Running the Claude prompt hook or the advisor CLI is not a model session, and both run in a sandboxed socket directory here.\n49: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T024 checks it.\n50: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, edits `AGENTS.md`, renames a command or edits a file under `.skilled/commands/create/`.\n51: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n52: \n53: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n54: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`), T002 (`$SP/typed-before.json`). Read-only for the repository.\n55: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that phases 005, 008 and 010 are committed, because they edit `AR`, `CA`, `ST`, `SCH`, `DSY`, `IG` and `RA`.\n56: - Wave 1: T003 (`DB`, `CA`), T004 (`SRM`, `SRMT`), T005 (`SCH`), T006 (`MBC`), T007 (`IG`).\n57: - Wave 2: T008 (`CA`, `CAT`), T009 (`ST`, `STT`), T010 (`DSY`), T011 (`SRR`).\n58: - Wave 3: T012 (`FU`, `FT`), T013 (`AR`, `ART`), T014 (`HT`).\n59: - Wave 4: T015 (`CAP`), T016 (`AR`).\n60: - Wave 5: T017 (`BASE`), T018 (`RA`), T019 (`CBR`).\n61: - Wave 6, one at a time because both start a sandboxed daemon: T020 (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`), T021 (`$SP/brief-cost.json`).\n62: - Verification, one at a time: T022, T023, T024, T025.\n63: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n68: ## Phase 1: Setup\n69: \n70: - [x] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3, .metrics.typed_reach_top3, .metrics.agreement, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached, .attachedMetrics.plain'`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `DBC`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VST`, `node --test $SRMT`, `node $SRM --check` and `DCT`, and write each command with its last output line or exit code into `before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` and a fourth no higher than 5, `TC` and `PY` exit 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `node $SRM --check` exits 0 and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 15. When a gate is red here, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because a red before number cannot be held.\n71: - [x] T002 Record each typed row's state (`$SP/typed-before.json`): run `node $CAP | jq '[.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key | endswith(\"-typed-01\")) | {id: .key, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1}] as $t | .attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [$t[] | . + {attached: ($a[.id] // null)}]' > $SP/typed-before.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 44, `jq '[.[] | select(.top1)] | length' $SP/typed-before.json` prints 24 and `jq -r '.[] | keys[]' $SP/typed-before.json | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' '` prints `attached id top top1 `.\n72: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n77: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n78: \n79: ### Wave 1\n80: - [ ] T003 [P] Generate one table of every tracked command (`DB`, `CA`). In `CA`, above `// BEGIN GENERATED COMMAND ROWS`, add `export interface TrackedCommand { readonly policy: 'run' | 'start' | 'ask'; readonly ownerSkillId: string; readonly scorerRow: boolean; }` with a comment saying the table covers every tracked command, whether or not its bridge is active, so a typed command always has a policy and an owner. In `DB`'s `renderCommandRows(entries, policies)`, build a third map over every entry, with no enabled filter: key `entryRunnable(entry)`, value `{ policy: runnablePolicy(runnable, policies), ownerSkillId: entry.skillId, scorerRow: entry.runtime.typescript.enabled === true }`. Throw `[command-bridges] Conflicting tracked command <runnable>` when one runnable repeats with a different owner or policy. Render it after the `COMMAND_POLICIES` block as `export const TRACKED_COMMANDS: Readonly<Record<string, TrackedCommand>> = {` with one line per key in sorted order, shaped `  '<runnable>': { policy: '<policy>', ownerSkillId: '<owner>', scorerRow: <true|false> },`, and `};`. Run `node $DB`. Do not edit the generated block by hand. Verify: `DBC` prints `fresh`, `rg -c \"scorerRow: (true|false) \\},$\" $CA` prints at least 44, `rg -n \"'/create:goal': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'sk-doc', scorerRow: false \\},\" $CA` and `rg -n \"'/speckit:resume': \\{ policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit', scorerRow: true \\},\" $CA` each print one line, `TC` exits 0, `npx vitest run tests/command-bridges-drift-guard.vitest.ts tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- $ADV/scripts/skill_advisor.py $ADV/lib/scorer/projection.ts` prints nothing.\n81: - [ ] T004 [P] Add a command visibility count and a collapse guard to the mirror check (`SRM`, `SRMT`). Add `const COLLAPSE_ALLOWLIST = new Set(['create/skill.md'])` with a comment saying Claude Code reads a command folder holding `skill.md` as one skill and hides its siblings, and the existing case is kept until the commands are renamed. Add and export `commandVisibility(expectedLinks)`: it takes the `claude-commands` links whose path below `.claude/commands/` holds a folder, counts them as `total`, and counts Claude's `visible` as one per folder that holds `skill.md` plus every command in the other folders. Cursor's `visible` is `total`, because Cursor uses flat names. Add and export `collapseDrift(expectedLinks)`, which returns `COLLAPSE <folder>/skill.md hides <n> sibling commands on Claude Code` for each folder holding `skill.md` whose path is not in the allowlist. In `checkMirrors`, add those lines to `drift`, and before the PASS line print `[runtime-mirror-sync] visibility claude <visible>/<total>, cursor <total>/<total>`. Keep the PASS line as it is. In `SRMT`, add one case where synthetic links for `create/skill.md`, `create/readme.md` and `deep/review.md` give `{ claude: { visible: 2, total: 3 }, cursor: { visible: 3, total: 3 } }` and no drift, and one where an added `design/skill.md` beside `design/chart.md` gives `['COLLAPSE design/skill.md hides 1 sibling commands on Claude Code']`. Verify: `node --test $SRMT` reports `fail 0`, `node $SRM --check` prints a line containing `visibility claude 32/44, cursor 44/44` and exits 0, and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n82: - [ ] T005 [P] Add `hookHealth` to the status output schema (`SCH`). In `AdvisorStatusOutputSchema`, after `embeddingsHealth`, add `hookHealth: z.object({ invocations: z.number().int().positive(), rollingP95Ms: z.number().nonnegative(), rollingFailOpenRate: z.number().min(0).max(1), rollingCacheHitRate: z.number().min(0).max(1) }).strict().optional()`. Verify: `TC` exits 0, `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $SCH` prints 1 and `npx vitest run tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV` reports 0 failed.\n83: - [ ] T006 [P] Create `MBC`. `node $MBC [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` reads `CORPUS` under `--root` (default the current directory), keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, and with `--rows` keeps only the named ids, exiting 2 with `{\"error\":\"holdout_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for a row whose split is `holdout` and `{\"error\":\"unknown_row\",\"id\":\"<id>\"}` for an id it cannot find, before it spawns anything. It makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-cost-'))`, and for each row pipes `{\"prompt\":<prompt>,\"cwd\":<root>,\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"cost-<id>\"}` into `node <root>/$HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to that directory and a 30,000 ms timeout. It takes `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` (or the empty string), and records `bytes` as its UTF-8 length and `estimatedTokens` as `Math.ceil(bytes / 4)`, the renderer's own four characters per token. It prints one JSON object `{ rows: [{ id, bytes, estimatedTokens }], totalBytes, medianBytes, maxBytes }` and never prints a prompt. At the end it sends `SIGTERM` only to a process whose command line names that directory and removes the directory. Verify: `node --check $MBC` exits 0, `node $MBC --rows cmd-speckit-save-typed-01 | jq -c '.rows[0] | [.id, (.bytes > 0), (.estimatedTokens == ((.bytes + 3) / 4 | floor))]'` prints `[\"cmd-speckit-save-typed-01\",true,true]`, `node $MBC --rows \"$(jq -r 'select(.split==\"holdout\") | .id' $CORPUS | head -1)\"` exits 2 and prints `holdout_row`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n84: - [ ] T007 [P] In `IG`, under `### Check the Install`, add one paragraph after the paragraph about Codex hook approval: to see the advisor answer a prompt, run `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the utils folder\"}' --format json | jq -r '.data.recommendations[0].skillId'`, which prints one skill id such as `sk-doc`, and when it prints nothing or `null`, run `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild`. Re-read the section first, because phase 008 also edits section 2. Verify: `rg -c \"advisor_recommend --json\" $IG` prints 1, and the documented command prints one line that is neither empty nor `null`.\n85: \n86: ### Wave 2\n87: - [ ] T008 [P] Add two typed command helpers (`CA`, `CAT`). `typedCommandIn(prompt)` lowercases the prompt and returns the longest `TRACKED_COMMANDS` key found at a position where the character before it is absent, whitespace, a backtick, a quote or `(`, and the character after it is absent or not a word character, colon or hyphen. It returns undefined when none is found. `typedCommandForTop(topSkillId, prompt)` takes that key and returns `{ command, policy, ownerSkillId }` from its row when `topSkillId` is the row's `ownerSkillId` or `commandRowFor(topSkillId)?.command` equals the key, and undefined otherwise. In `CAT`, add cases: `/create:skill-parent deploy-hub with three modes` gives `/create:skill-parent`, `/create:sk` gives undefined, `see docs/create:goal.md` gives undefined, `typedCommandForTop('system-spec-kit', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives `{ command: '/speckit:resume', policy: 'start', ownerSkillId: 'system-spec-kit' }`, `typedCommandForTop('command-speckit-resume', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives the same and `typedCommandForTop('sk-doc', '/speckit:resume cache work')` gives undefined. Verify: `VCA` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n88: - [ ] T009 [P] Report hook health in the status (`ST`, `STT`). Run after phase 008, which edits `readSemanticLaneHealth` and `handleAdvisorStatus` in the same file. In `readAdvisorStatus`, in the `output` object of the success path (the one inside `try`, right after `laneWeights: DEFAULT_SCORER_WEIGHTS,`), add `hookHealth` from `readAdvisorHookHealthSection(workspaceRoot)` (`$ADV/lib/metrics.ts`) when its `lastInvocations` is not empty: `invocations` is the length of `lastInvocations` and the three rolling fields copy over. Leave the error path as it is. Add a comment saying the p95 counts cache-hit invocations only. In `STT`, add one case that writes two valid diagnostic records with `persistAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord` for a temporary workspace root and reads `hookHealth.invocations` 2, and one case for a root with no records that reads no `hookHealth` key. Verify: `VST` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n89: - [ ] T010 [P] Show hook health under the setup advisor leg (`DSY`). Run after phase 008, which adds an embedder leg after this one. In the single list item that starts `- \"Leg advisor:`, append before its closing quote: ` When data.hookHealth is present, show hook p95 <rollingP95Ms> ms over <invocations> prompts and fail-open <rollingFailOpenRate as a percent> under the leg. It never changes the leg's status.` Change nothing else. Verify: `rg -c \"hookHealth\" $DSY` prints 1 and `DCT` reports `fail 0`.\n90: - [ ] T011 [P] In `SRR` section 5, add two sentences after the check command: the check also prints how many tracked commands each runtime shows, and it fails when a command folder other than `create/` holds a `skill.md`, because Claude Code would then hide that folder's other commands. Verify: `rg -n \"skill.md\" $SRR` prints at least one line in section 5.\n91: \n92: ### Wave 3\n93: - [ ] T012 [P] Put the owner first for a typed command with no scorer row (`FU`, `FT`). Import `typedCommandIn` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from `../command-attach.js`. Export `rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower)`: when `typedCommandIn(promptLower)` names a row with `scorerRow` false and that row's owner is at an index above 0 with `passes_threshold` true, return the list with the owner moved to index 0 and every other entry in its order. Otherwise return `ranked` unchanged. In `scoreAdvisorPrompt`, call `ranked = rankTypedCommandOwnerFirst(ranked, promptLower);` right after the `applyDoctorHelperHandoff` statement and before `const passing`. Add a comment saying a typed command with no row of its own can only reach the reader through its owner, so the owner must lead. In `FT`, add cases on synthetic lists: `/create:goal x` with `sk-doc` passing at index 1 moves it first, the same with `sk-doc` not passing changes nothing, `/speckit:save x` (a command with a scorer row) changes nothing and a prompt with no slash changes nothing. Verify: `VFT` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n94: - [ ] T013 [P] Attach a typed command to the top recommendation (`AR`, `ART`). Import `typedCommandForTop` from `../lib/command-attach.js` and export `attachTypedCommand(output, prompt)`: when `output.recommendations[0]` exists and `typedCommandForTop(top.skillId, prompt)` returns a row, return `AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse` of the output with the first recommendation's `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId` set from that row, replacing any compiled route command. Otherwise return `output` itself. In `computeRecommendationOutput`, wrap both final returns, the cached one and the last one, as `return attachTypedCommand(<the existing expression>, input.prompt);`. Add a comment saying a typed command is the reader's own choice, so it outranks a routed mode. In `ART`, add cases: a top `system-spec-kit` with prompt `/speckit:resume cache work` carries `/speckit:resume`, `start` and `system-spec-kit`, a top `sk-doc` already carrying `/create:readme` with prompt `/create:goal retrofit` carries `/create:goal` and `start`, a top `mcp-tooling` with that prompt returns the same object and a prompt with no slash returns the same object. Verify: `VART` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n95: - [ ] T014 [P] Create `HT`. For every key of `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, render `renderAdvisorBrief` with one passing `skill` recommendation for the row's owner that carries the key as `command`, its `policy` and its `ownerSkillId`, freshness `live` and `metrics: { tokenCap: 80 }`, in the shape `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` uses. Do the same for every id in `COMMAND_ROWS` as a `command` recommendation. Take the head before `DIRECTIVES_LABEL` and assert that `Buffer.byteLength(head, 'utf8')` is at most `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT`, that the head does not end with `...` and that it contains `<command> (<policy>)`. Define `HEAD_BYTE_LIMIT` as `80 * 4` with a comment naming the renderer's default token cap and its four characters per token. Add one case where a synthetic 330-character command fails the same check. Verify: `VHT` reports 0 failed and at least 45 passed tests.\n96: \n97: ### Wave 4\n98: - [ ] T015 Add the typed block to the command capture (`CAP`). Import `typedCommandForTop` and `TRACKED_COMMANDS` from the built attach module the script already loads. For each row with `row_type` `typed`, compute `delivered` as `typedCommandForTop(topId, row.prompt)?.command ?? attached`, the same precedence as the handler, and `policy` as `TRACKED_COMMANDS[delivered]?.policy ?? null`. Count `own` when `top1Hit`, else `attached` when `delivered` equals the expected command and `policy` is not null, in a tally built with `emptyAttachedTally` and `addAttachedRow`. Add `typed: attachedTallyJson(...)` to `attachedMetrics`, and `typedRows`, keyed by row id in sorted order, each `{ expected_command, top, carried, command, policy }`, where `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null. Write no prompt text. Leave every existing key and its computation unchanged. Verify: after `BLD`, `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed'` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `node $CAP | jq -c '[.attachedMetrics.typedRows[] | keys] | unique'` prints `[[\"carried\",\"command\",\"expected_command\",\"policy\",\"top\"]]` and `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n99: - [ ] T016 Take a hub-attached command's policy from the new table (`AR`). In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace the spread that reads `COMMAND_POLICIES` with `...(Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command) ? { policy: TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy } : {})`, import `TRACKED_COMMANDS` and drop `COMMAND_POLICIES` from the import when nothing else uses it. Verify: `rg -c \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" $AR` prints nothing or 0, `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS\\[command\\]\" $AR` prints 1, `TC` exits 0 and `VART` reports 0 failed.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 5\n102: - [ ] T017 Rebuild and recapture the command baseline (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.typed' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}`, `jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}`, `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `jq '.metrics.slash_variant.reached_top3' $BASE` prints at least the value in `$SP/before.txt`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain' $BASE` equals the line in `$SP/before.txt` and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n103: - [ ] T018 [P] In `RA`, describe `attachedMetrics.typed` and `attachedMetrics.typedRows`, the rule that a typed command travels on its owner, and `measure-brief-cost.mjs` with its flags, its output keys and its sandbox. Verify: `rg -c \"attachedMetrics.typed|typedRows|measure-brief-cost\" $RA` prints at least 3.\n104: - [ ] T019 [P] In `CBR`, describe `TRACKED_COMMANDS`: one row per bridge entry with its policy, its owner and `scorerRow`, generated whether or not the bridge is active. Verify: `rg -c \"TRACKED_COMMANDS|scorerRow\" $CBR` prints at least 2.\n105: \n106: ### Wave 6\n107: - [ ] T020 Check the real heads (`$SP/typed-heads.mjs`, `$SP/typed-heads.json`). Write a script that reads `CORPUS`, keeps rows with `row_type` `typed` and `split` `none`, makes one directory with `mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'ssa-typed-'))`, pipes each row's payload (the shape of T006, session id `typed-<id>`) into `node $HOOK` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` set to it, takes the first line of `additionalContext` and reads it with `parseOffer` from `OF`. It writes `[{ id, offeredCommand, offeredPolicy, commandMatches, policyPresent, labelAgrees }]` to `typed-heads.json`, where `commandMatches` compares the `/<namespace>:<name>` part of the offer with the row's `expected_command`, `policyPresent` is true for `run`, `start` or `ask` and `labelAgrees` compares the policy with the row's `expected_policy`. It stops and removes its sandbox as T006 does and prints no prompt. Verify: `jq '[.[] | select(.commandMatches and .policyPresent)] | length' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints 44, `jq -r '[.[] | select(.labelAgrees | not) | .id] | join(\",\")' $SP/typed-heads.json` prints `cmd-design-extract-typed-01,cmd-doctor-env-typed-01,cmd-doctor-update-typed-01` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-typed-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n108: - [ ] T021 Measure the brief cost (`$SP/brief-cost.json`). Run `node $MBC > $SP/brief-cost.json`. Verify: `jq '.rows | length' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints 44, `jq '.maxBytes > 0 and .medianBytes > 0' $SP/brief-cost.json` prints `true` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-cost-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n109: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n114: ## Phase 3: Verification\n115: \n116: - [ ] T022 Run every floor. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt` and no lower than `[159,50,20]` with the fourth no higher than 5, `TC` exits 0, `PY` exits 0, `DBC` prints `fresh`, `RS`, `PAR`, `VCA`, `VFT`, `VART`, `VHT` and `VST` report 0 failed, `node --test $SRMT` and `DCT` report `fail 0` and `node $SRM --check` exits 0. Write the outputs beside the before lines in `$SP/after.txt`.\n117: - [ ] T023 Read hook health live. Make one directory with `D=$(mktemp -d \"$TMPDIR/ssa-status-XXXXXX\")`, run `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR=\"$D\" node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_status --workspace-root \"$PWD\" --format json --no-warm-only | jq -r '.data.hookHealth.rollingP95Ms | type'`, then stop only a process whose command line names `$D` and remove `$D`. The Claude prompt hook runs in T020 and T021 wrote the diagnostic records it reads. Verify: the command prints `number`, and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-status-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing.\n118: - [ ] T024 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled INSTALL-SKILLED.md AGENTS.md` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n119: - [ ] T025 Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n120: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n125: ## Completion Criteria\n126: \n127: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n128: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n129: - [ ] The committed baseline reads `attachedMetrics.typed.combined` 44 of 44 and every floor holds against `scratch/before.txt`\n130: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n131: \n132: ---\n133: \n134: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n135: ## Cross-References\n136: \n137: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n138: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n139: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n140: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research.md`\n141: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## Verification Checklist\n146: \n147: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n148: ## Verification Protocol\n149: \n150: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n151: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n152: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n153: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n154: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n155: \n156: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n157: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n162: ## Pre-Implementation\n163: \n164: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the typed row state are in `scratch/` (T001, T002).\n165: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The coordinator confirmed phases 005, 008 and 010 are committed before wave 1 started.\n166: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n167: \n168: ---\n169: \n170: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n171: ## Code Quality\n172: \n173: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `npm run typecheck` in `ADV` exits 0.\n174: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id (T024).\n175: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The generated block is written by the deriver only, and its check prints `fresh` (T003, T022).\n176: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] A prompt with no typed command leaves the recommendation unchanged (T012, T013).\n177: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n178: \n179: ---\n180: \n181: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n182: ## Testing Checklist\n183: \n184: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds (T022).\n185: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] The baseline reads 44 of 44 typed and the slice test passes (T017).\n186: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] The parity test and the Python test pass (T022).\n187: - [ ] CHK-023 [P1] The real heads of all 44 typed rows carry their command and a policy (T020).\n188: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n189: \n190: ---\n191: \n192: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n193: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n194: \n195: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] The gap is class `delivery`: the scorer finds the owner, and the command is lost between the scorer and the head.\n196: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Every reader of the policy table is listed: `rg -n \"COMMAND_POLICIES|TRACKED_COMMANDS\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'`.\n197: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P1] Both return paths of the recommend handler apply the typed step (T013).\n198: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on.\n199: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n204: ## Security\n205: \n206: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task reads or prints holdout prompt text, and the cost script refuses a holdout row (T006).\n207: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] No task writes to the home folder or installs anything (T024).\n208: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n213: ## Documentation\n214: \n215: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same numbers.\n216: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The install guide, the setup asset and the three READMEs match the code (T007, T010, T011, T018, T019).\n217: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n222: ## File Organization\n223: \n224: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Probes, measurements and the hook check live in `scratch/` only.\n225: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n230: ## Verification Summary\n231: \n232: | Category | Total | Verified |\n233: |----------|-------|----------|\n234: | P0 Items | 12 | 0/12 |\n235: | P1 Items | 7 | 0/7 |\n236: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n237: \n238: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n239: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/011-typed-command-delivery-and-guards/tasks.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794539477375,"end":1794539477773}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794539479292,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"id":"prt_1d2e360fc001AuzfSPhDzWVN63","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e34eac0019Sj3lYZDnnA5nB","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5f19ea3f623d5a46b2efc65cec641ff89ac5c739","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":407,"output":162,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":17152,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794539481454,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"id":"prt_1d2e3696e001G3HiZc1llUq7W2","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e36102001nS4yZnysJsfT37","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c0024f0919db02cf7ec10de0792839fbc11169db"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794539484041,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2e37250001bdPlPkBGFBl3X5","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e36102001nS4yZnysJsfT37","type":"tool","id":"call_5d28a043cbe144e98e8be27e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794539483728,"end":1794539484041}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794539484045,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2e36eae001LbdABBQ9VCytaT","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e36102001nS4yZnysJsfT37","type":"tool","id":"call_41df94275ad34ea9badc58bb","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs, lines 1-413\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Sync Runtime Mirrors\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Claude, Cursor, Devin and OpenCode discover agents and commands by file convention,\n6: // each at its own path and in its own shape. Rather than fork the content, every\n7: // entry is a symlink back to one canonical file, so a mirror cannot drift from\n8: // what it mirrors. This script owns those symlink trees.\n9: //\n10: // A real file sitting where a symlink belongs is the failure this guards: it\n11: // still works today and silently stops tracking the source tomorrow. That is\n12: // reported as STALE, never quietly accepted.\n13: //\n14: // Hook mirrors are derived from each runtime's own hook config rather than a\n15: // hand-kept list, because the config is what the runtime actually executes; a\n16: // list would be one more thing to forget to update.\n17: \n18: 'use strict';\n19: \n20: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n21: // 1. IMPORTS\n22: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n23: \n24: const fs = require('node:fs');\n25: const { execFileSync } = require('node:child_process');\n26: const path = require('node:path');\n27: const { isCanonicalMirrorExcluded, isRuntimeNativeCommand } = require('./command-scope.cjs');\n28: \n29: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n30: // 2. CONSTANTS\n31: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n32: \n33: const { findRepoRoot } = require('@spec-kit/shared/workspace/repo-root.mjs');\n34: const REPO_ROOT = findRepoRoot(__dirname);\n35: const EXCLUDED_COMMAND_DIRS = new Set(['assets', 'scripts', 'fixtures']);\n36: \n37: // Cursor and Devin parse the Claude agent dialect (`tools:`), not OpenCode's\n38: // `permission:` block, so agents deliberately source from .claude/agents.\n39: // Commands have no dialect split and source from .skilled directly.\n40: const CLAUDE_AGENTS = '.claude/agents';\n41: \n42: // Agents that exist only because Claude Code resolves a subagent by exact name and\n43: // lets a user or project file replace its built-in. No other runtime has that\n44: // built-in, and a mirrored copy would register a second, unrelated agent there.\n45: // Keep in step with the same list in the /doctor agent-roster check.\n46: const CLAUDE_ONLY_AGENTS = new Set(['Explore']);\n47: const CLAUDE_COMMANDS = '.claude/commands';\n48: const CANONICAL_COMMANDS = '.skilled/commands';\n49: // OpenCode reads the canonical tree in its own shape, so it mirrors each top-level\n50: // entry rather than each command file, and keeps its native command beside them.\n51: const OPENCODE_COMMANDS = '.opencode/commands';\n52: // Command roots that must be real directories of links, never one link to the tree.\n53: const REAL_COMMAND_ROOTS = [CLAUDE_COMMANDS, OPENCODE_COMMANDS];\n54: \n55: const HOOK_CONFIGS = [\n56:   { runtime: 'claude', config: '.claude/settings.json', mirror: '.claude/hooks' },\n57:   { runtime: 'codex', config: '.codex/hooks.json', mirror: '.codex/hooks' },\n58:   { runtime: 'cursor', config: '.cursor/hooks.json', mirror: '.cursor/hooks' },\n59:   { runtime: 'devin', config: '.devin/hooks.v1.json', mirror: '.devin/hooks' },\n60: ];\n61: \n62: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n63: // 3. HELPERS\n64: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n65: \n66: function parseArguments(argv) {\n67:   if (argv.length === 0) {\n68:     return { check: false };\n69:   }\n70:   if (argv.length === 1 && argv[0] === '--check') {\n71:     return { check: true };\n72:   }\n73:   throw new Error('Usage: node sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs [--check]');\n74: }\n75: \n76: function listAgentNames(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n77:   const dir = path.join(repoRoot, CLAUDE_AGENTS);\n78:   if (!fs.existsSync(dir)) {\n79:     throw new Error(`No canonical agents found at ${CLAUDE_AGENTS}`);\n80:   }\n81:   return fs.readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true })\n82:     .filter((e) => e.isFile() && e.name.endsWith('.md'))\n83:     .map((e) => e.name.slice(0, -3))\n84:     .filter((name) => !CLAUDE_ONLY_AGENTS.has(name))\n85:     .sort();\n86: }\n87: \n88: function listCommandPaths(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n89:   const root = path.join(repoRoot, CANONICAL_COMMANDS);\n90:   if (!fs.existsSync(root)) {\n91:     throw new Error(`No canonical commands found at ${CANONICAL_COMMANDS}`);\n92:   }\n93:   const found = [];\n94:   const walk = (current) => {\n95:     for (const entry of fs.readdirSync(current, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n96:       const absolute = path.join(current, entry.name);\n97:       if (entry.isDirectory()) {\n98:         if (!EXCLUDED_COMMAND_DIRS.has(entry.name)) walk(absolute);\n99:         continue;\n100:       }\n101:       if (!entry.isFile() || !entry.name.endsWith('.md')) continue;\n102:       if (entry.name === 'README.md' || entry.name.endsWith('.contract.md')) continue;\n103:       found.push(path.relative(root, absolute).split(path.sep).join('/'));\n104:     }\n105:   };\n106:   walk(root);\n107:   return found.sort();\n108: }\n109: \n110: function isGitIgnored(repoPath, repoRoot) {\n111:   try {\n112:     execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'check-ignore', '-q', '--', repoPath], {\n113:       cwd: repoRoot,\n114:       stdio: 'ignore',\n115:     });\n116:     return true;\n117:   } catch (error) {\n118:     if (error.status === 1) return false;\n119:     throw new Error(`Unable to check Git ignore state for ${repoPath}: ${error.message}`);\n120:   }\n121: }\n122: \n123: // Git-ignored mirror paths cannot be represented in a fresh checkout.\n124: function listCommandRootEntries(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n125:   return fs.readdirSync(path.join(repoRoot, CANONICAL_COMMANDS))\n126:     .filter((name) => !name.startsWith('.') && !name.startsWith('local-'))\n127:     .filter((name) => !isGitIgnored(`${OPENCODE_COMMANDS}/${name}`, repoRoot))\n128:     .sort();\n129: }\n130: \n131: // `create/agent.md` -> `create-agent`: both runtimes expose a flat namespace,\n132: // so the directory separator collapses into the name.\n133: function toFlatName(relativePath) {\n134:   return relativePath.replace(/\\.md$/, '').split('/').join('-');\n135: }\n136: \n137: // Every path the runtime's own config actually invokes is a path its discovery\n138: // mirror should expose. Reading the raw text keeps this indifferent to the four\n139: // mutually incompatible config shapes.\n140: // DSH loads one plugin bundle whose adapters folder holds a relative link per\n141: // adapter, the shape .pi/extensions has. Each real adapter lives in the hook\n142: // library's <concern>/dsh/ folder, or in its owning skill with an index link\n143: // there, so the expectations derive from those folders and a new adapter needs\n144: // no list edit. A checkout without the bundle expects no links.\n145: const DSH_BUNDLE_ENTRY = '.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs';\n146: const DSH_ADAPTERS = '.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters';\n147: const HOOK_LIBRARY = '.skilled/hooks';\n148: \n149: /** The DSH adapter links a checkout's hook library implies, each aimed at its real file. */\n150: function dshAdapterLinks(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n151:   if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(repoRoot, DSH_BUNDLE_ENTRY))) return [];\n152:   const realRoot = fs.realpathSync(repoRoot);\n153:   const links = [];\n154:   for (const concern of fs.readdirSync(path.join(repoRoot, HOOK_LIBRARY), { withFileTypes: true })) {\n155:     if (!concern.isDirectory()) continue;\n156:     const dir = path.join(repoRoot, HOOK_LIBRARY, concern.name, 'dsh');\n157:     if (!fs.existsSync(dir)) continue;\n158:     for (const name of fs.readdirSync(dir).sort()) {\n159:       if (!name.endsWith('.mjs') || name.endsWith('.test.mjs')) continue;\n160:       const entry = path.join(dir, name);\n161:       // An index link names the skill-owned file; the adapter link aims straight at it.\n162:       let real;\n163:       try {\n164:         real = fs.realpathSync(entry);\n165:       } catch {\n166:         real = entry;\n167:       }\n168:       const source = path.relative(real.startsWith(realRoot) ? realRoot : repoRoot, real).split(path.sep).join('/');\n169:       links.push({ tree: 'dsh-adapters', mirror: `${DSH_ADAPTERS}/${name}`, source });\n170:     }\n171:   }\n172:   return links.sort((left, right) => left.mirror.localeCompare(right.mirror));\n173: }\n174: \n175: function hookSourcesFromConfig(configRelative, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n176:   const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, configRelative);\n177:   if (!fs.existsSync(absolute)) return [];\n178:   const raw = fs.readFileSync(absolute, 'utf8');\n179:   const matches = raw.match(/\\.skilled\\/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+?\\.(?:mjs|cjs|js|sh|py)/g) || [];\n180:   return [...new Set(matches)].sort();\n181: }\n182: \n183: // Each entry: { mirror: repo-relative path of the symlink, source: repo-relative target }\n184: /** Derive mirror expectations using the ignore rules of the given repository. */\n185: function buildExpectedLinks(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n186:   const links = [];\n187:   const agents = listAgentNames(repoRoot);\n188:   const commands = listCommandPaths(repoRoot);\n189: \n190:   for (const name of agents) {\n191:     const source = `${CLAUDE_AGENTS}/${name}.md`;\n192:     links.push({ tree: 'cursor-agents', mirror: `.cursor/agents/${name}.md`, source });\n193:     links.push({ tree: 'devin-agents', mirror: `.devin/agents/${name}/AGENT.md`, source });\n194:   }\n195: \n196:   for (const relativePath of commands) {\n197:     // Runtime-exclusive commands (the goal triggers) are not cross-mirrored; each\n198:     // runtime carries only its own hand-authored native command instead.\n199:     if (isCanonicalMirrorExcluded(relativePath)) continue;\n200:     const source = `${CANONICAL_COMMANDS}/${relativePath}`;\n201:     const flat = toFlatName(relativePath);\n202:     links.push({ tree: 'claude-commands', mirror: `${CLAUDE_COMMANDS}/${relativePath}`, source });\n203:     links.push({ tree: 'cursor-commands', mirror: `.cursor/commands/${flat}.md`, source });\n204:   }\n205: \n206:   for (const name of listCommandRootEntries(repoRoot)) {\n207:     links.push({ tree: 'opencode-commands', mirror: `${OPENCODE_COMMANDS}/${name}`, source: `${CANONICAL_COMMANDS}/${name}` });\n208:   }\n209: \n210:   for (const { runtime, config, mirror } of HOOK_CONFIGS) {\n211:     for (const source of hookSourcesFromConfig(config, repoRoot)) {\n212:       links.push({ tree: `${runtime}-hooks`, mirror: `${mirror}/${path.posix.basename(source)}`, source });\n213:     }\n214:   }\n215: \n216:   links.push(...dshAdapterLinks(repoRoot));\n217: \n218:   if (links.length === 0) throw new Error('No expected mirrors were derived');\n219:   return links;\n220: }\n221: \n222: // Mirrors are relative symlinks so the tree stays valid when the repo moves.\n223: function expectedTarget(mirrorRelative, sourceRelative, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n224:   return path.relative(\n225:     path.dirname(path.join(repoRoot, mirrorRelative)),\n226:     path.join(repoRoot, sourceRelative),\n227:   );\n228: }\n229: \n230: /** Build output is regenerated, never committed, so its absence is not drift. */\n231: function isBuildArtifact(target) {\n232:   return target.split('/').includes('dist');\n233: }\n234: \n235: function inspect(link, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n236:   const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, link.mirror);\n237:   let stat = null;\n238:   try {\n239:     stat = fs.lstatSync(absolute);\n240:   } catch (err) {\n241:     if (err.code !== 'ENOENT') throw err;\n242:     return { state: 'MISSING' };\n243:   }\n244:   if (!stat.isSymbolicLink()) return { state: 'STALE', why: 'not a symlink (silent fork risk)' };\n245:   const want = expectedTarget(link.mirror, link.source, repoRoot);\n246:   const actual = fs.readlinkSync(absolute);\n247:   if (actual !== want) return { state: 'STALE', why: `points at ${actual}, expected ${want}` };\n248:   // A mirror aimed at the right place but resolving to nothing is either a deleted source\n249:   // or an unbuilt artifact, and only the first is drift. Build output is gitignored, so a\n250:   // fresh checkout has none of it — treating that as drift makes this gate fail on every\n251:   // run in CI while passing on any machine that happens to have built, which reports\n252:   // build state as mirror integrity and trains readers to ignore the result.\n253:   if (!fs.existsSync(absolute) && !isBuildArtifact(want)) {\n254:     return { state: 'STALE', why: 'broken symlink (source is gone)' };\n255:   }\n256:   return { state: 'OK' };\n257: }\n258: \n259: /** Find unignored mirror entries that no canonical source expects. */\n260: function findOrphans(expectedLinks, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n261:   const expectedByDir = new Map();\n262:   for (const link of expectedLinks) {\n263:     const dir = path.posix.dirname(link.mirror);\n264:     if (!expectedByDir.has(dir)) expectedByDir.set(dir, new Set());\n265:     expectedByDir.get(dir).add(path.posix.basename(link.mirror));\n266:   }\n267: \n268:   const orphans = [];\n269:   for (const [dir, names] of expectedByDir) {\n270:     const absoluteDir = path.join(repoRoot, dir);\n271:     if (!fs.existsSync(absoluteDir)) continue;\n272:     for (const entry of fs.readdirSync(absoluteDir, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n273:       if (entry.name.startsWith('.')) continue;\n274:       if ((entry.name === 'README.md' || entry.name === 'README.txt') && !names.has(entry.name)) continue;\n275:       // A local-* entry is private to one checkout and git-ignored, so it is neither\n276:       // drift nor an orphan to prune.\n277:       if (entry.name.startsWith('local-')) continue;\n278:       // Nested trees (devin) hold one dir per item; flat trees hold files/symlinks.\n279:       if (entry.isDirectory()) continue;\n280:       // A runtime's own hand-authored native command is deliberately not a mirror.\n281:       if (isRuntimeNativeCommand(dir, entry.name)) continue;\n282:       const mirror = path.posix.join(dir, entry.name);\n283:       if (!names.has(entry.name) && !isGitIgnored(mirror, repoRoot)) orphans.push(mirror);\n284:     }\n285:   }\n286: \n287:   // Devin nests each item in its own directory, so an orphan there is a whole dir.\n288:   for (const parent of ['.devin/agents', '.devin/skills']) {\n289:     const absoluteParent = path.join(repoRoot, parent);\n290:     if (!fs.existsSync(absoluteParent)) continue;\n291:     // A parent that is itself a symlink points at a canonical tree, not a mirror\n292:     // tree; pruning through it would delete the canonical files.\n293:     if (fs.lstatSync(absoluteParent).isSymbolicLink()) continue;\n294:     const leaf = parent.endsWith('agents') ? 'AGENT.md' : 'SKILL.md';\n295:     const wanted = new Set(\n296:       expectedLinks\n297:         .filter((l) => l.mirror.startsWith(`${parent}/`))\n298:         .map((l) => l.mirror.split('/')[2]),\n299:     );\n300:     for (const entry of fs.readdirSync(absoluteParent, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n301:       if (!entry.isDirectory() || entry.name.startsWith('.')) continue;\n302:       // A hand-authored native skill in this tree is not a mirror.\n303:       if (isRuntimeNativeCommand(parent, entry.name)) continue;\n304:       if (!wanted.has(entry.name)) orphans.push(`${parent}/${entry.name}/${leaf}`);\n305:     }\n306:   }\n307: \n308:   return orphans.sort();\n309: }\n310: \n311: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n312: // 4. SYNC EXECUTION\n313: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n314: \n315: /** Check for missing or stale links under the repository used to derive them. */\n316: function checkMirrors(expectedLinks, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n317:   const drift = [];\n318:   for (const commandsRoot of REAL_COMMAND_ROOTS) {\n319:     try {\n320:       const rootStat = fs.lstatSync(path.join(repoRoot, commandsRoot));\n321:       if (!rootStat.isDirectory() || rootStat.isSymbolicLink()) {\n322:         drift.push(`STALE ${commandsRoot} — must be a real filtered command directory`);\n323:       }\n324:     } catch (error) {\n325:       if (error?.code === 'ENOENT') drift.push(`MISSING ${commandsRoot}`);\n326:       else throw error;\n327:     }\n328:   }\n329:   for (const link of expectedLinks) {\n330:     const result = inspect(link, repoRoot);\n331:     if (result.state === 'MISSING') drift.push(`MISSING ${link.mirror}`);\n332:     else if (result.state === 'STALE') drift.push(`STALE ${link.mirror} — ${result.why}`);\n333:   }\n334:   for (const orphan of findOrphans(expectedLinks, repoRoot)) drift.push(`EXTRA ${orphan}`);\n335: \n336:   if (drift.length > 0) {\n337:     console.error('[runtime-mirror-sync] Drift detected:');\n338:     for (const item of drift) console.error(`[runtime-mirror-sync] ${item}`);\n339:     return false;\n340:   }\n341: \n342:   const trees = new Set(expectedLinks.map((l) => l.tree));\n343:   console.log(`[runtime-mirror-sync] PASS: ${expectedLinks.length} mirrors across ${trees.size} trees are in sync.`);\n344:   return true;\n345: }\n346: \n347: /** Apply expected links under the repository used to derive them. */\n348: function writeMirrors(expectedLinks, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n349:   let changed = 0;\n350:   for (const commandsRoot of REAL_COMMAND_ROOTS) {\n351:     const absoluteRoot = path.join(repoRoot, commandsRoot);\n352:     try {\n353:       const rootStat = fs.lstatSync(absoluteRoot);\n354:       if (rootStat.isSymbolicLink()) {\n355:         fs.unlinkSync(absoluteRoot);\n356:       } else if (!rootStat.isDirectory()) {\n357:         throw new Error(`${commandsRoot} exists but is not a directory`);\n358:       }\n359:     } catch (error) {\n360:       if (error?.code !== 'ENOENT') throw error;\n361:     }\n362:     fs.mkdirSync(absoluteRoot, { recursive: true });\n363:   }\n364: \n365:   for (const link of expectedLinks) {\n366:     const result = inspect(link, repoRoot);\n367:     if (result.state === 'OK') continue;\n368:     const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, link.mirror);\n369:     fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(absolute), { recursive: true });\n370:     if (result.state === 'STALE') fs.rmSync(absolute, { force: true });\n371:     fs.symlinkSync(expectedTarget(link.mirror, link.source, repoRoot), absolute);\n372:     changed += 1;\n373:   }\n374: \n375:   let removed = 0;\n376:   for (const orphan of findOrphans(expectedLinks, repoRoot)) {\n377:     const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, orphan);\n378:     fs.rmSync(absolute, { force: true });\n379:     // Devin's per-item directory is meaningless once its only entry is gone.\n380:     const parent = path.dirname(absolute);\n381:     if (/\\.devin\\/(agents|skills)\\//.test(orphan) && fs.existsSync(parent) && fs.readdirSync(parent).length === 0) {\n382:       fs.rmdirSync(parent);\n383:     }\n384:     removed += 1;\n385:   }\n386: \n387:   console.log(`[runtime-mirror-sync] Linked ${changed}, removed ${removed}, of ${expectedLinks.length} expected mirrors.`);\n388: }\n389: \n390: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n391: // 5. ENTRY POINT\n392: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n393: \n394: function main() {\n395:   const options = parseArguments(process.argv.slice(2));\n396:   const expectedLinks = buildExpectedLinks();\n397:   if (options.check) {\n398:     if (!checkMirrors(expectedLinks)) process.exitCode = 1;\n399:     return;\n400:   }\n401:   writeMirrors(expectedLinks);\n402: }\n403: \n404: if (require.main === module) {\n405:   try {\n406:     main();\n407:   } catch (error) {\n408:     console.error(`[runtime-mirror-sync] ERROR: ${error.message}`);\n409:     process.exitCode = 1;\n410:   }\n411: }\n412: \n413: module.exports = { buildExpectedLinks, checkMirrors, dshAdapterLinks, findOrphans, writeMirrors };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs, lines 1-413\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Sync Runtime Mirrors\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Claude, Cursor, Devin and OpenCode discover agents and commands by file convention,\n6: // each at its own path and in its own shape. Rather than fork the content, every\n7: // entry is a symlink back to one canonical file, so a mirror cannot drift from\n8: // what it mirrors. This script owns those symlink trees.\n9: //\n10: // A real file sitting where a symlink belongs is the failure this guards: it\n11: // still works today and silently stops tracking the source tomorrow. That is\n12: // reported as STALE, never quietly accepted.\n13: //\n14: // Hook mirrors are derived from each runtime's own hook config rather than a\n15: // hand-kept list, because the config is what the runtime actually executes; a\n16: // list would be one more thing to forget to update.\n17: \n18: 'use strict';\n19: \n20: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n21: // 1. IMPORTS\n22: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n23: \n24: const fs = require('node:fs');\n25: const { execFileSync } = require('node:child_process');\n26: const path = require('node:path');\n27: const { isCanonicalMirrorExcluded, isRuntimeNativeCommand } = require('./command-scope.cjs');\n28: \n29: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n30: // 2. CONSTANTS\n31: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n32: \n33: const { findRepoRoot } = require('@spec-kit/shared/workspace/repo-root.mjs');\n34: const REPO_ROOT = findRepoRoot(__dirname);\n35: const EXCLUDED_COMMAND_DIRS = new Set(['assets', 'scripts', 'fixtures']);\n36: \n37: // Cursor and Devin parse the Claude agent dialect (`tools:`), not OpenCode's\n38: // `permission:` block, so agents deliberately source from .claude/agents.\n39: // Commands have no dialect split and source from .skilled directly.\n40: const CLAUDE_AGENTS = '.claude/agents';\n41: \n42: // Agents that exist only because Claude Code resolves a subagent by exact name and\n43: // lets a user or project file replace its built-in. No other runtime has that\n44: // built-in, and a mirrored copy would register a second, unrelated agent there.\n45: // Keep in step with the same list in the /doctor agent-roster check.\n46: const CLAUDE_ONLY_AGENTS = new Set(['Explore']);\n47: const CLAUDE_COMMANDS = '.claude/commands';\n48: const CANONICAL_COMMANDS = '.skilled/commands';\n49: // OpenCode reads the canonical tree in its own shape, so it mirrors each top-level\n50: // entry rather than each command file, and keeps its native command beside them.\n51: const OPENCODE_COMMANDS = '.opencode/commands';\n52: // Command roots that must be real directories of links, never one link to the tree.\n53: const REAL_COMMAND_ROOTS = [CLAUDE_COMMANDS, OPENCODE_COMMANDS];\n54: \n55: const HOOK_CONFIGS = [\n56:   { runtime: 'claude', config: '.claude/settings.json', mirror: '.claude/hooks' },\n57:   { runtime: 'codex', config: '.codex/hooks.json', mirror: '.codex/hooks' },\n58:   { runtime: 'cursor', config: '.cursor/hooks.json', mirror: '.cursor/hooks' },\n59:   { runtime: 'devin', config: '.devin/hooks.v1.json', mirror: '.devin/hooks' },\n60: ];\n61: \n62: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n63: // 3. HELPERS\n64: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n65: \n66: function parseArguments(argv) {\n67:   if (argv.length === 0) {\n68:     return { check: false };\n69:   }\n70:   if (argv.length === 1 && argv[0] === '--check') {\n71:     return { check: true };\n72:   }\n73:   throw new Error('Usage: node sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs [--check]');\n74: }\n75: \n76: function listAgentNames(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n77:   const dir = path.join(repoRoot, CLAUDE_AGENTS);\n78:   if (!fs.existsSync(dir)) {\n79:     throw new Error(`No canonical agents found at ${CLAUDE_AGENTS}`);\n80:   }\n81:   return fs.readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true })\n82:     .filter((e) => e.isFile() && e.name.endsWith('.md'))\n83:     .map((e) => e.name.slice(0, -3))\n84:     .filter((name) => !CLAUDE_ONLY_AGENTS.has(name))\n85:     .sort();\n86: }\n87: \n88: function listCommandPaths(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n89:   const root = path.join(repoRoot, CANONICAL_COMMANDS);\n90:   if (!fs.existsSync(root)) {\n91:     throw new Error(`No canonical commands found at ${CANONICAL_COMMANDS}`);\n92:   }\n93:   const found = [];\n94:   const walk = (current) => {\n95:     for (const entry of fs.readdirSync(current, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n96:       const absolute = path.join(current, entry.name);\n97:       if (entry.isDirectory()) {\n98:         if (!EXCLUDED_COMMAND_DIRS.has(entry.name)) walk(absolute);\n99:         continue;\n100:       }\n101:       if (!entry.isFile() || !entry.name.endsWith('.md')) continue;\n102:       if (entry.name === 'README.md' || entry.name.endsWith('.contract.md')) continue;\n103:       found.push(path.relative(root, absolute).split(path.sep).join('/'));\n104:     }\n105:   };\n106:   walk(root);\n107:   return found.sort();\n108: }\n109: \n110: function isGitIgnored(repoPath, repoRoot) {\n111:   try {\n112:     execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'check-ignore', '-q', '--', repoPath], {\n113:       cwd: repoRoot,\n114:       stdio: 'ignore',\n115:     });\n116:     return true;\n117:   } catch (error) {\n118:     if (error.status === 1) return false;\n119:     throw new Error(`Unable to check Git ignore state for ${repoPath}: ${error.message}`);\n120:   }\n121: }\n122: \n123: // Git-ignored mirror paths cannot be represented in a fresh checkout.\n124: function listCommandRootEntries(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n125:   return fs.readdirSync(path.join(repoRoot, CANONICAL_COMMANDS))\n126:     .filter((name) => !name.startsWith('.') && !name.startsWith('local-'))\n127:     .filter((name) => !isGitIgnored(`${OPENCODE_COMMANDS}/${name}`, repoRoot))\n128:     .sort();\n129: }\n130: \n131: // `create/agent.md` -> `create-agent`: both runtimes expose a flat namespace,\n132: // so the directory separator collapses into the name.\n133: function toFlatName(relativePath) {\n134:   return relativePath.replace(/\\.md$/, '').split('/').join('-');\n135: }\n136: \n137: // Every path the runtime's own config actually invokes is a path its discovery\n138: // mirror should expose. Reading the raw text keeps this indifferent to the four\n139: // mutually incompatible config shapes.\n140: // DSH loads one plugin bundle whose adapters folder holds a relative link per\n141: // adapter, the shape .pi/extensions has. Each real adapter lives in the hook\n142: // library's <concern>/dsh/ folder, or in its owning skill with an index link\n143: // there, so the expectations derive from those folders and a new adapter needs\n144: // no list edit. A checkout without the bundle expects no links.\n145: const DSH_BUNDLE_ENTRY = '.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs';\n146: const DSH_ADAPTERS = '.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters';\n147: const HOOK_LIBRARY = '.skilled/hooks';\n148: \n149: /** The DSH adapter links a checkout's hook library implies, each aimed at its real file. */\n150: function dshAdapterLinks(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n151:   if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(repoRoot, DSH_BUNDLE_ENTRY))) return [];\n152:   const realRoot = fs.realpathSync(repoRoot);\n153:   const links = [];\n154:   for (const concern of fs.readdirSync(path.join(repoRoot, HOOK_LIBRARY), { withFileTypes: true })) {\n155:     if (!concern.isDirectory()) continue;\n156:     const dir = path.join(repoRoot, HOOK_LIBRARY, concern.name, 'dsh');\n157:     if (!fs.existsSync(dir)) continue;\n158:     for (const name of fs.readdirSync(dir).sort()) {\n159:       if (!name.endsWith('.mjs') || name.endsWith('.test.mjs')) continue;\n160:       const entry = path.join(dir, name);\n161:       // An index link names the skill-owned file; the adapter link aims straight at it.\n162:       let real;\n163:       try {\n164:         real = fs.realpathSync(entry);\n165:       } catch {\n166:         real = entry;\n167:       }\n168:       const source = path.relative(real.startsWith(realRoot) ? realRoot : repoRoot, real).split(path.sep).join('/');\n169:       links.push({ tree: 'dsh-adapters', mirror: `${DSH_ADAPTERS}/${name}`, source });\n170:     }\n171:   }\n172:   return links.sort((left, right) => left.mirror.localeCompare(right.mirror));\n173: }\n174: \n175: function hookSourcesFromConfig(configRelative, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n176:   const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, configRelative);\n177:   if (!fs.existsSync(absolute)) return [];\n178:   const raw = fs.readFileSync(absolute, 'utf8');\n179:   const matches = raw.match(/\\.skilled\\/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+?\\.(?:mjs|cjs|js|sh|py)/g) || [];\n180:   return [...new Set(matches)].sort();\n181: }\n182: \n183: // Each entry: { mirror: repo-relative path of the symlink, source: repo-relative target }\n184: /** Derive mirror expectations using the ignore rules of the given repository. */\n185: function buildExpectedLinks(repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n186:   const links = [];\n187:   const agents = listAgentNames(repoRoot);\n188:   const commands = listCommandPaths(repoRoot);\n189: \n190:   for (const name of agents) {\n191:     const source = `${CLAUDE_AGENTS}/${name}.md`;\n192:     links.push({ tree: 'cursor-agents', mirror: `.cursor/agents/${name}.md`, source });\n193:     links.push({ tree: 'devin-agents', mirror: `.devin/agents/${name}/AGENT.md`, source });\n194:   }\n195: \n196:   for (const relativePath of commands) {\n197:     // Runtime-exclusive commands (the goal triggers) are not cross-mirrored; each\n198:     // runtime carries only its own hand-authored native command instead.\n199:     if (isCanonicalMirrorExcluded(relativePath)) continue;\n200:     const source = `${CANONICAL_COMMANDS}/${relativePath}`;\n201:     const flat = toFlatName(relativePath);\n202:     links.push({ tree: 'claude-commands', mirror: `${CLAUDE_COMMANDS}/${relativePath}`, source });\n203:     links.push({ tree: 'cursor-commands', mirror: `.cursor/commands/${flat}.md`, source });\n204:   }\n205: \n206:   for (const name of listCommandRootEntries(repoRoot)) {\n207:     links.push({ tree: 'opencode-commands', mirror: `${OPENCODE_COMMANDS}/${name}`, source: `${CANONICAL_COMMANDS}/${name}` });\n208:   }\n209: \n210:   for (const { runtime, config, mirror } of HOOK_CONFIGS) {\n211:     for (const source of hookSourcesFromConfig(config, repoRoot)) {\n212:       links.push({ tree: `${runtime}-hooks`, mirror: `${mirror}/${path.posix.basename(source)}`, source });\n213:     }\n214:   }\n215: \n216:   links.push(...dshAdapterLinks(repoRoot));\n217: \n218:   if (links.length === 0) throw new Error('No expected mirrors were derived');\n219:   return links;\n220: }\n221: \n222: // Mirrors are relative symlinks so the tree stays valid when the repo moves.\n223: function expectedTarget(mirrorRelative, sourceRelative, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n224:   return path.relative(\n225:     path.dirname(path.join(repoRoot, mirrorRelative)),\n226:     path.join(repoRoot, sourceRelative),\n227:   );\n228: }\n229: \n230: /** Build output is regenerated, never committed, so its absence is not drift. */\n231: function isBuildArtifact(target) {\n232:   return target.split('/').includes('dist');\n233: }\n234: \n235: function inspect(link, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n236:   const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, link.mirror);\n237:   let stat = null;\n238:   try {\n239:     stat = fs.lstatSync(absolute);\n240:   } catch (err) {\n241:     if (err.code !== 'ENOENT') throw err;\n242:     return { state: 'MISSING' };\n243:   }\n244:   if (!stat.isSymbolicLink()) return { state: 'STALE', why: 'not a symlink (silent fork risk)' };\n245:   const want = expectedTarget(link.mirror, link.source, repoRoot);\n246:   const actual = fs.readlinkSync(absolute);\n247:   if (actual !== want) return { state: 'STALE', why: `points at ${actual}, expected ${want}` };\n248:   // A mirror aimed at the right place but resolving to nothing is either a deleted source\n249:   // or an unbuilt artifact, and only the first is drift. Build output is gitignored, so a\n250:   // fresh checkout has none of it — treating that as drift makes this gate fail on every\n251:   // run in CI while passing on any machine that happens to have built, which reports\n252:   // build state as mirror integrity and trains readers to ignore the result.\n253:   if (!fs.existsSync(absolute) && !isBuildArtifact(want)) {\n254:     return { state: 'STALE', why: 'broken symlink (source is gone)' };\n255:   }\n256:   return { state: 'OK' };\n257: }\n258: \n259: /** Find unignored mirror entries that no canonical source expects. */\n260: function findOrphans(expectedLinks, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n261:   const expectedByDir = new Map();\n262:   for (const link of expectedLinks) {\n263:     const dir = path.posix.dirname(link.mirror);\n264:     if (!expectedByDir.has(dir)) expectedByDir.set(dir, new Set());\n265:     expectedByDir.get(dir).add(path.posix.basename(link.mirror));\n266:   }\n267: \n268:   const orphans = [];\n269:   for (const [dir, names] of expectedByDir) {\n270:     const absoluteDir = path.join(repoRoot, dir);\n271:     if (!fs.existsSync(absoluteDir)) continue;\n272:     for (const entry of fs.readdirSync(absoluteDir, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n273:       if (entry.name.startsWith('.')) continue;\n274:       if ((entry.name === 'README.md' || entry.name === 'README.txt') && !names.has(entry.name)) continue;\n275:       // A local-* entry is private to one checkout and git-ignored, so it is neither\n276:       // drift nor an orphan to prune.\n277:       if (entry.name.startsWith('local-')) continue;\n278:       // Nested trees (devin) hold one dir per item; flat trees hold files/symlinks.\n279:       if (entry.isDirectory()) continue;\n280:       // A runtime's own hand-authored native command is deliberately not a mirror.\n281:       if (isRuntimeNativeCommand(dir, entry.name)) continue;\n282:       const mirror = path.posix.join(dir, entry.name);\n283:       if (!names.has(entry.name) && !isGitIgnored(mirror, repoRoot)) orphans.push(mirror);\n284:     }\n285:   }\n286: \n287:   // Devin nests each item in its own directory, so an orphan there is a whole dir.\n288:   for (const parent of ['.devin/agents', '.devin/skills']) {\n289:     const absoluteParent = path.join(repoRoot, parent);\n290:     if (!fs.existsSync(absoluteParent)) continue;\n291:     // A parent that is itself a symlink points at a canonical tree, not a mirror\n292:     // tree; pruning through it would delete the canonical files.\n293:     if (fs.lstatSync(absoluteParent).isSymbolicLink()) continue;\n294:     const leaf = parent.endsWith('agents') ? 'AGENT.md' : 'SKILL.md';\n295:     const wanted = new Set(\n296:       expectedLinks\n297:         .filter((l) => l.mirror.startsWith(`${parent}/`))\n298:         .map((l) => l.mirror.split('/')[2]),\n299:     );\n300:     for (const entry of fs.readdirSync(absoluteParent, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n301:       if (!entry.isDirectory() || entry.name.startsWith('.')) continue;\n302:       // A hand-authored native skill in this tree is not a mirror.\n303:       if (isRuntimeNativeCommand(parent, entry.name)) continue;\n304:       if (!wanted.has(entry.name)) orphans.push(`${parent}/${entry.name}/${leaf}`);\n305:     }\n306:   }\n307: \n308:   return orphans.sort();\n309: }\n310: \n311: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n312: // 4. SYNC EXECUTION\n313: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n314: \n315: /** Check for missing or stale links under the repository used to derive them. */\n316: function checkMirrors(expectedLinks, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n317:   const drift = [];\n318:   for (const commandsRoot of REAL_COMMAND_ROOTS) {\n319:     try {\n320:       const rootStat = fs.lstatSync(path.join(repoRoot, commandsRoot));\n321:       if (!rootStat.isDirectory() || rootStat.isSymbolicLink()) {\n322:         drift.push(`STALE ${commandsRoot} — must be a real filtered command directory`);\n323:       }\n324:     } catch (error) {\n325:       if (error?.code === 'ENOENT') drift.push(`MISSING ${commandsRoot}`);\n326:       else throw error;\n327:     }\n328:   }\n329:   for (const link of expectedLinks) {\n330:     const result = inspect(link, repoRoot);\n331:     if (result.state === 'MISSING') drift.push(`MISSING ${link.mirror}`);\n332:     else if (result.state === 'STALE') drift.push(`STALE ${link.mirror} — ${result.why}`);\n333:   }\n334:   for (const orphan of findOrphans(expectedLinks, repoRoot)) drift.push(`EXTRA ${orphan}`);\n335: \n336:   if (drift.length > 0) {\n337:     console.error('[runtime-mirror-sync] Drift detected:');\n338:     for (const item of drift) console.error(`[runtime-mirror-sync] ${item}`);\n339:     return false;\n340:   }\n341: \n342:   const trees = new Set(expectedLinks.map((l) => l.tree));\n343:   console.log(`[runtime-mirror-sync] PASS: ${expectedLinks.length} mirrors across ${trees.size} trees are in sync.`);\n344:   return true;\n345: }\n346: \n347: /** Apply expected links under the repository used to derive them. */\n348: function writeMirrors(expectedLinks, repoRoot = REPO_ROOT) {\n349:   let changed = 0;\n350:   for (const commandsRoot of REAL_COMMAND_ROOTS) {\n351:     const absoluteRoot = path.join(repoRoot, commandsRoot);\n352:     try {\n353:       const rootStat = fs.lstatSync(absoluteRoot);\n354:       if (rootStat.isSymbolicLink()) {\n355:         fs.unlinkSync(absoluteRoot);\n356:       } else if (!rootStat.isDirectory()) {\n357:         throw new Error(`${commandsRoot} exists but is not a directory`);\n358:       }\n359:     } catch (error) {\n360:       if (error?.code !== 'ENOENT') throw error;\n361:     }\n362:     fs.mkdirSync(absoluteRoot, { recursive: true });\n363:   }\n364: \n365:   for (const link of expectedLinks) {\n366:     const result = inspect(link, repoRoot);\n367:     if (result.state === 'OK') continue;\n368:     const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, link.mirror);\n369:     fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(absolute), { recursive: true });\n370:     if (result.state === 'STALE') fs.rmSync(absolute, { force: true });\n371:     fs.symlinkSync(expectedTarget(link.mirror, link.source, repoRoot), absolute);\n372:     changed += 1;\n373:   }\n374: \n375:   let removed = 0;\n376:   for (const orphan of findOrphans(expectedLinks, repoRoot)) {\n377:     const absolute = path.join(repoRoot, orphan);\n378:     fs.rmSync(absolute, { force: true });\n379:     // Devin's per-item directory is meaningless once its only entry is gone.\n380:     const parent = path.dirname(absolute);\n381:     if (/\\.devin\\/(agents|skills)\\//.test(orphan) && fs.existsSync(parent) && fs.readdirSync(parent).length === 0) {\n382:       fs.rmdirSync(parent);\n383:     }\n384:     removed += 1;\n385:   }\n386: \n387:   console.log(`[runtime-mirror-sync] Linked ${changed}, removed ${removed}, of ${expectedLinks.length} expected mirrors.`);\n388: }\n389: \n390: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n391: // 5. ENTRY POINT\n392: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n393: \n394: function main() {\n395:   const options = parseArguments(process.argv.slice(2));\n396:   const expectedLinks = buildExpectedLinks();\n397:   if (options.check) {\n398:     if (!checkMirrors(expectedLinks)) process.exitCode = 1;\n399:     return;\n400:   }\n401:   writeMirrors(expectedLinks);\n402: }\n403: \n404: if (require.main === module) {\n405:   try {\n406:     main();\n407:   } catch (error) {\n408:     console.error(`[runtime-mirror-sync] ERROR: ${error.message}`);\n409:     process.exitCode = 1;\n410:   }\n411: }\n412: \n413: module.exports = { buildExpectedLinks, checkMirrors, dshAdapterLinks, findOrphans, writeMirrors };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794539482798,"end":1794539484045}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794539484048,"sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2e370a20016buSN5iRXXeaNz","sessionID":"ses_e2d1e00b7ffeXYNr5JwmmzZPgF","messageID":"msg_1d2e36102001nS4yZnysJsfT37","type":"tool","id":"call_d59ab8deebb1474c81fd2696","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs, lines 1-260\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Sync Runtime Mirrors - orphan detection tests\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \n5: import assert from 'node:assert/strict';\n6: import {\n7:   existsSync,\n8:   lstatSync,\n9:   mkdirSync,\n10:   mkdtempSync,\n11:   rmdirSync,\n12:   rmSync,\n13:   symlinkSync,\n14:   unlinkSync,\n15:   writeFileSync,\n16: } from 'node:fs';\n17: import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\n18: import { createRequire } from 'node:module';\n19: import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\n20: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n21: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n22: import test, { afterEach } from 'node:test';\n23: \n24: const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);\n25: // RUNTIME_MIRROR_TEST_ROOT pins the fixture to a folder a caller wants to inspect afterwards;\n26: // without it each run gets a fresh system temp folder, so a plain `node --test` still works.\n27: const scratchRoot = process.env.RUNTIME_MIRROR_TEST_ROOT || mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'sync-runtime-mirrors-'));\n28: const fixtureRoot = join(resolve(scratchRoot), 'sync-runtime-mirrors-fixtures');\n29: const {\n30:   buildExpectedLinks,\n31:   checkMirrors,\n32:   dshAdapterLinks,\n33:   findOrphans,\n34:   writeMirrors,\n35: } = require(join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..', 'runtime-mirrors', 'sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs'));\n36: \n37: const FIXTURE_FILES = [\n38:   '.gitignore',\n39:   'source.md',\n40:   'private/agent.md',\n41:   '.claude/agents/kept.md',\n42:   '.claude/agents/dropped.md',\n43:   '.claude/agents/local-file.md',\n44:   '.claude/agents/local-link.md',\n45:   '.claude/agents/fixture-agent.md',\n46:   '.claude/commands/public/command.md',\n47:   '.cursor/agents/fixture-agent.md',\n48:   '.cursor/commands/public-command.md',\n49:   '.devin/agents/fixture-agent/AGENT.md',\n50:   '.skilled/commands/README.txt',\n51:   '.skilled/commands/deep/command.md',\n52:   '.skilled/commands/assets/private-command.md',\n53:   '.skilled/commands/expected-root/command.md',\n54:   '.skilled/commands/scripts/tool.cjs',\n55:   '.opencode/commands/deep',\n56:   '.opencode/commands/README.txt',\n57:   '.opencode/commands/goal-opencode.md',\n58:   '.opencode/commands/stray.md',\n59:   '.opencode/commands/assets',\n60:   '.opencode/commands/scripts',\n61:   '.opencode/commands/expected-root',\n62: ];\n63: \n64: const FIXTURE_DIRECTORIES = [\n65:   'private',\n66:   '.claude/agents',\n67:   '.claude/commands/public',\n68:   '.claude/commands',\n69:   '.claude',\n70:   '.cursor/agents',\n71:   '.cursor/commands',\n72:   '.cursor',\n73:   '.devin/agents/fixture-agent',\n74:   '.devin/agents',\n75:   '.devin',\n76:   '.skilled/commands/deep',\n77:   '.skilled/commands/assets',\n78:   '.skilled/commands/expected-root',\n79:   '.skilled/commands/scripts',\n80:   '.skilled/commands',\n81:   '.skilled',\n82:   '.opencode/commands',\n83:   '.opencode',\n84: ];\n85: \n86: function removeScratchPath(relativePath) {\n87:   const absolutePath = join(fixtureRoot, relativePath);\n88:   try {\n89:     if (lstatSync(absolutePath).isDirectory()) rmdirSync(absolutePath);\n90:     else unlinkSync(absolutePath);\n91:   } catch (error) {\n92:     if (error.code !== 'ENOENT' && error.code !== 'ENOTEMPTY') throw error;\n93:   }\n94: }\n95: \n96: function clearFixture() {\n97:   for (const relativePath of FIXTURE_FILES) removeScratchPath(relativePath);\n98:   for (const relativePath of FIXTURE_DIRECTORIES) removeScratchPath(relativePath);\n99: }\n100: \n101: function startFixture() {\n102:   mkdirSync(fixtureRoot, { recursive: true });\n103:   if (!existsSync(join(fixtureRoot, '.git'))) {\n104:     execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'init'], {\n105:       cwd: fixtureRoot,\n106:       stdio: 'ignore',\n107:     });\n108:   }\n109:   execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'config', '--local', 'core.excludesFile', '/dev/null'], {\n110:     cwd: fixtureRoot,\n111:     stdio: 'ignore',\n112:   });\n113:   clearFixture();\n114:   return fixtureRoot;\n115: }\n116: \n117: function fixture() {\n118:   const root = startFixture();\n119:   const mirrorDir = join(root, '.claude', 'agents');\n120:   mkdirSync(mirrorDir, { recursive: true });\n121:   writeFileSync(join(root, 'source.md'), '# source\\n');\n122:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', 'source.md'), join(mirrorDir, 'kept.md'));\n123:   const expected = [{ mirror: '.claude/agents/kept.md', source: 'source.md' }];\n124:   return { root, mirrorDir, expected };\n125: }\n126: \n127: function commandRootFixture() {\n128:   const root = startFixture();\n129:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.gitignore'), '/.opencode/commands/assets\\n/.opencode/commands/scripts\\n');\n130:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.claude', 'agents'), { recursive: true });\n131:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.claude', 'commands'), { recursive: true });\n132:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.opencode', 'commands'), { recursive: true });\n133:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'assets'), { recursive: true });\n134:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'expected-root'), { recursive: true });\n135:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'scripts'), { recursive: true });\n136:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.claude', 'agents', 'fixture-agent.md'), '# agent\\n');\n137:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'assets', 'private-command.md'), '# private\\n');\n138:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'expected-root', 'command.md'), '# expected\\n');\n139:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'scripts', 'tool.cjs'), 'module.exports = {};\\n');\n140:   symlinkSync(\n141:     join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'scripts'),\n142:     join(root, '.opencode', 'commands', 'scripts'),\n143:   );\n144:   execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'add', '--force', '--', '.opencode/commands/scripts'], {\n145:     cwd: root,\n146:     stdio: 'ignore',\n147:   });\n148:   return root;\n149: }\n150: \n151: function captureConsole(method, action) {\n152:   const original = console[method];\n153:   const messages = [];\n154:   console[method] = (...args) => messages.push(args.join(' '));\n155:   try {\n156:     return { value: action(), messages };\n157:   } finally {\n158:     console[method] = original;\n159:   }\n160: }\n161: \n162: afterEach(clearFixture);\n163: \n164: test('reports an entry no canonical source expects as an orphan', () => {\n165:   const env = fixture();\n166:   writeFileSync(join(env.mirrorDir, 'dropped.md'), '# dropped\\n');\n167:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(env.expected, env.root), ['.claude/agents/dropped.md']);\n168: });\n169: \n170: test('never reports a private local-* entry as an orphan, as a file or as a link', () => {\n171:   const env = fixture();\n172:   writeFileSync(join(env.mirrorDir, 'local-file.md'), '# private\\n');\n173:   mkdirSync(join(env.root, 'private'), { recursive: true });\n174:   writeFileSync(join(env.root, 'private', 'agent.md'), '# private\\n');\n175:   symlinkSync(join(env.root, 'private', 'agent.md'), join(env.mirrorDir, 'local-link.md'));\n176:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(env.expected, env.root), []);\n177: });\n178: \n179: test('omits ignored command roots, keeps tracked roots and reports a missing OpenCode link', () => {\n180:   const root = commandRootFixture();\n181:   const expected = buildExpectedLinks(root);\n182:   const openCodeMirrors = expected\n183:     .filter((link) => link.tree === 'opencode-commands')\n184:     .map((link) => link.mirror);\n185:   assert.equal(openCodeMirrors.includes('.opencode/commands/assets'), false);\n186:   assert.equal(openCodeMirrors.includes('.opencode/commands/scripts'), true);\n187:   assert.equal(\n188:     execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'ls-files', '--error-unmatch', '.opencode/commands/scripts'], {\n189:       cwd: root,\n190:       encoding: 'utf8',\n191:     }).trim(),\n192:     '.opencode/commands/scripts',\n193:   );\n194: \n195:   const result = captureConsole('error', () => checkMirrors(expected, root));\n196:   assert.equal(result.value, false);\n197:   assert.equal(result.messages.some((line) => line.includes('MISSING .opencode/commands/expected-root')), true);\n198: });\n199: \n200: test('write sync leaves ignored roots uncreated and preserves an existing ignored link', () => {\n201:   const root = commandRootFixture();\n202:   const expected = buildExpectedLinks(root);\n203:   const ignoredLink = join(root, '.opencode', 'commands', 'assets');\n204: \n205:   captureConsole('log', () => writeMirrors(expected, root));\n206:   assert.equal(existsSync(ignoredLink), false);\n207:   assert.equal(lstatSync(join(root, '.opencode', 'commands', 'expected-root')).isSymbolicLink(), true);\n208: \n209:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'assets'), ignoredLink);\n210:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(expected, root), []);\n211:   captureConsole('log', () => writeMirrors(expected, root));\n212:   assert.equal(lstatSync(ignoredLink).isSymbolicLink(), true);\n213: });\n214: \n215: test('keeps the OpenCode native goal command and a mirrored README, but flags a stray entry', () => {\n216:   const root = startFixture();\n217:   const commandsDir = join(root, '.opencode', 'commands');\n218:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'deep'), { recursive: true });\n219:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'README.txt'), 'commands\\n');\n220:   mkdirSync(commandsDir, { recursive: true });\n221:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'deep'), join(commandsDir, 'deep'));\n222:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'README.txt'), join(commandsDir, 'README.txt'));\n223:   writeFileSync(join(commandsDir, 'goal-opencode.md'), '# goal\\n');\n224:   writeFileSync(join(commandsDir, 'stray.md'), '# stray\\n');\n225:   const expected = [\n226:     { mirror: '.opencode/commands/deep', source: '.skilled/commands/deep' },\n227:     { mirror: '.opencode/commands/README.txt', source: '.skilled/commands/README.txt' },\n228:   ];\n229:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(expected, root), ['.opencode/commands/stray.md']);\n230: });\n231: \n232: test('derives each DSH adapter link from the hook library and flags a real file or a missing link', () => {\n233:   const root = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'sync-runtime-mirrors-dsh-'));\n234:   try {\n235:     const write = (relativePath, text = 'export {};\\n') => {\n236:       mkdirSync(dirname(join(root, relativePath)), { recursive: true });\n237:       writeFileSync(join(root, relativePath), text);\n238:     };\n239:     write('.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs');\n240:     write('.skilled/hooks/overlay-guard/dsh/overlay-guard.mjs');\n241:     write('.skilled/hooks/overlay-guard/dsh/overlay-guard.test.mjs');\n242:     write('.skilled/skills/owner/hooks/dsh/owned.mjs');\n243:     mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled/hooks/owned/dsh'), { recursive: true });\n244:     symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '..', 'skills', 'owner', 'hooks', 'dsh', 'owned.mjs'), join(root, '.skilled/hooks/owned/dsh/owned.mjs'));\n245: \n246:     const links = dshAdapterLinks(root);\n247:     assert.deepEqual(links.map(({ mirror, source }) => ({ mirror, source })), [\n248:       { mirror: '.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/overlay-guard.mjs', source: '.skilled/hooks/overlay-guard/dsh/overlay-guard.mjs' },\n249:       { mirror: '.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/owned.mjs', source: '.skilled/skills/owner/hooks/dsh/owned.mjs' },\n250:     ]);\n251: \n252:     write('.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/overlay-guard.mjs');\n253:     const result = captureConsole('error', () => checkMirrors(links, root));\n254:     assert.equal(result.value, false);\n255:     assert.equal(result.messages.some((line) => line.includes('STALE .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/overlay-guard.mjs')), true);\n256:     assert.equal(result.messages.some((line) => line.includes('MISSING .dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/owned.mjs')), true);\n257:   } finally {\n258:     rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });\n259:   }\n260: });","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/sync-runtime-mirrors.test.mjs, lines 1-260\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Sync Runtime Mirrors - orphan detection tests\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \n5: import assert from 'node:assert/strict';\n6: import {\n7:   existsSync,\n8:   lstatSync,\n9:   mkdirSync,\n10:   mkdtempSync,\n11:   rmdirSync,\n12:   rmSync,\n13:   symlinkSync,\n14:   unlinkSync,\n15:   writeFileSync,\n16: } from 'node:fs';\n17: import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\n18: import { createRequire } from 'node:module';\n19: import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\n20: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n21: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n22: import test, { afterEach } from 'node:test';\n23: \n24: const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);\n25: // RUNTIME_MIRROR_TEST_ROOT pins the fixture to a folder a caller wants to inspect afterwards;\n26: // without it each run gets a fresh system temp folder, so a plain `node --test` still works.\n27: const scratchRoot = process.env.RUNTIME_MIRROR_TEST_ROOT || mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'sync-runtime-mirrors-'));\n28: const fixtureRoot = join(resolve(scratchRoot), 'sync-runtime-mirrors-fixtures');\n29: const {\n30:   buildExpectedLinks,\n31:   checkMirrors,\n32:   dshAdapterLinks,\n33:   findOrphans,\n34:   writeMirrors,\n35: } = require(join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..', 'runtime-mirrors', 'sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs'));\n36: \n37: const FIXTURE_FILES = [\n38:   '.gitignore',\n39:   'source.md',\n40:   'private/agent.md',\n41:   '.claude/agents/kept.md',\n42:   '.claude/agents/dropped.md',\n43:   '.claude/agents/local-file.md',\n44:   '.claude/agents/local-link.md',\n45:   '.claude/agents/fixture-agent.md',\n46:   '.claude/commands/public/command.md',\n47:   '.cursor/agents/fixture-agent.md',\n48:   '.cursor/commands/public-command.md',\n49:   '.devin/agents/fixture-agent/AGENT.md',\n50:   '.skilled/commands/README.txt',\n51:   '.skilled/commands/deep/command.md',\n52:   '.skilled/commands/assets/private-command.md',\n53:   '.skilled/commands/expected-root/command.md',\n54:   '.skilled/commands/scripts/tool.cjs',\n55:   '.opencode/commands/deep',\n56:   '.opencode/commands/README.txt',\n57:   '.opencode/commands/goal-opencode.md',\n58:   '.opencode/commands/stray.md',\n59:   '.opencode/commands/assets',\n60:   '.opencode/commands/scripts',\n61:   '.opencode/commands/expected-root',\n62: ];\n63: \n64: const FIXTURE_DIRECTORIES = [\n65:   'private',\n66:   '.claude/agents',\n67:   '.claude/commands/public',\n68:   '.claude/commands',\n69:   '.claude',\n70:   '.cursor/agents',\n71:   '.cursor/commands',\n72:   '.cursor',\n73:   '.devin/agents/fixture-agent',\n74:   '.devin/agents',\n75:   '.devin',\n76:   '.skilled/commands/deep',\n77:   '.skilled/commands/assets',\n78:   '.skilled/commands/expected-root',\n79:   '.skilled/commands/scripts',\n80:   '.skilled/commands',\n81:   '.skilled',\n82:   '.opencode/commands',\n83:   '.opencode',\n84: ];\n85: \n86: function removeScratchPath(relativePath) {\n87:   const absolutePath = join(fixtureRoot, relativePath);\n88:   try {\n89:     if (lstatSync(absolutePath).isDirectory()) rmdirSync(absolutePath);\n90:     else unlinkSync(absolutePath);\n91:   } catch (error) {\n92:     if (error.code !== 'ENOENT' && error.code !== 'ENOTEMPTY') throw error;\n93:   }\n94: }\n95: \n96: function clearFixture() {\n97:   for (const relativePath of FIXTURE_FILES) removeScratchPath(relativePath);\n98:   for (const relativePath of FIXTURE_DIRECTORIES) removeScratchPath(relativePath);\n99: }\n100: \n101: function startFixture() {\n102:   mkdirSync(fixtureRoot, { recursive: true });\n103:   if (!existsSync(join(fixtureRoot, '.git'))) {\n104:     execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'init'], {\n105:       cwd: fixtureRoot,\n106:       stdio: 'ignore',\n107:     });\n108:   }\n109:   execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'config', '--local', 'core.excludesFile', '/dev/null'], {\n110:     cwd: fixtureRoot,\n111:     stdio: 'ignore',\n112:   });\n113:   clearFixture();\n114:   return fixtureRoot;\n115: }\n116: \n117: function fixture() {\n118:   const root = startFixture();\n119:   const mirrorDir = join(root, '.claude', 'agents');\n120:   mkdirSync(mirrorDir, { recursive: true });\n121:   writeFileSync(join(root, 'source.md'), '# source\\n');\n122:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', 'source.md'), join(mirrorDir, 'kept.md'));\n123:   const expected = [{ mirror: '.claude/agents/kept.md', source: 'source.md' }];\n124:   return { root, mirrorDir, expected };\n125: }\n126: \n127: function commandRootFixture() {\n128:   const root = startFixture();\n129:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.gitignore'), '/.opencode/commands/assets\\n/.opencode/commands/scripts\\n');\n130:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.claude', 'agents'), { recursive: true });\n131:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.claude', 'commands'), { recursive: true });\n132:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.opencode', 'commands'), { recursive: true });\n133:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'assets'), { recursive: true });\n134:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'expected-root'), { recursive: true });\n135:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'scripts'), { recursive: true });\n136:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.claude', 'agents', 'fixture-agent.md'), '# agent\\n');\n137:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'assets', 'private-command.md'), '# private\\n');\n138:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'expected-root', 'command.md'), '# expected\\n');\n139:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'scripts', 'tool.cjs'), 'module.exports = {};\\n');\n140:   symlinkSync(\n141:     join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'scripts'),\n142:     join(root, '.opencode', 'commands', 'scripts'),\n143:   );\n144:   execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'add', '--force', '--', '.opencode/commands/scripts'], {\n145:     cwd: root,\n146:     stdio: 'ignore',\n147:   });\n148:   return root;\n149: }\n150: \n151: function captureConsole(method, action) {\n152:   const original = console[method];\n153:   const messages = [];\n154:   console[method] = (...args) => messages.push(args.join(' '));\n155:   try {\n156:     return { value: action(), messages };\n157:   } finally {\n158:     console[method] = original;\n159:   }\n160: }\n161: \n162: afterEach(clearFixture);\n163: \n164: test('reports an entry no canonical source expects as an orphan', () => {\n165:   const env = fixture();\n166:   writeFileSync(join(env.mirrorDir, 'dropped.md'), '# dropped\\n');\n167:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(env.expected, env.root), ['.claude/agents/dropped.md']);\n168: });\n169: \n170: test('never reports a private local-* entry as an orphan, as a file or as a link', () => {\n171:   const env = fixture();\n172:   writeFileSync(join(env.mirrorDir, 'local-file.md'), '# private\\n');\n173:   mkdirSync(join(env.root, 'private'), { recursive: true });\n174:   writeFileSync(join(env.root, 'private', 'agent.md'), '# private\\n');\n175:   symlinkSync(join(env.root, 'private', 'agent.md'), join(env.mirrorDir, 'local-link.md'));\n176:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(env.expected, env.root), []);\n177: });\n178: \n179: test('omits ignored command roots, keeps tracked roots and reports a missing OpenCode link', () => {\n180:   const root = commandRootFixture();\n181:   const expected = buildExpectedLinks(root);\n182:   const openCodeMirrors = expected\n183:     .filter((link) => link.tree === 'opencode-commands')\n184:     .map((link) => link.mirror);\n185:   assert.equal(openCodeMirrors.includes('.opencode/commands/assets'), false);\n186:   assert.equal(openCodeMirrors.includes('.opencode/commands/scripts'), true);\n187:   assert.equal(\n188:     execFileSync('git', ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'ls-files', '--error-unmatch', '.opencode/commands/scripts'], {\n189:       cwd: root,\n190:       encoding: 'utf8',\n191:     }).trim(),\n192:     '.opencode/commands/scripts',\n193:   );\n194: \n195:   const result = captureConsole('error', () => checkMirrors(expected, root));\n196:   assert.equal(result.value, false);\n197:   assert.equal(result.messages.some((line) => line.includes('MISSING .opencode/commands/expected-root')), true);\n198: });\n199: \n200: test('write sync leaves ignored roots uncreated and preserves an existing ignored link', () => {\n201:   const root = commandRootFixture();\n202:   const expected = buildExpectedLinks(root);\n203:   const ignoredLink = join(root, '.opencode', 'commands', 'assets');\n204: \n205:   captureConsole('log', () => writeMirrors(expected, root));\n206:   assert.equal(existsSync(ignoredLink), false);\n207:   assert.equal(lstatSync(join(root, '.opencode', 'commands', 'expected-root')).isSymbolicLink(), true);\n208: \n209:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'assets'), ignoredLink);\n210:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(expected, root), []);\n211:   captureConsole('log', () => writeMirrors(expected, root));\n212:   assert.equal(lstatSync(ignoredLink).isSymbolicLink(), true);\n213: });\n214: \n215: test('keeps the OpenCode native goal command and a mirrored README, but flags a stray entry', () => {\n216:   const root = startFixture();\n217:   const commandsDir = join(root, '.opencode', 'commands');\n218:   mkdirSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'deep'), { recursive: true });\n219:   writeFileSync(join(root, '.skilled', 'commands', 'README.txt'), 'commands\\n');\n220:   mkdirSync(commandsDir, { recursive: true });\n221:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'deep'), join(commandsDir, 'deep'));\n222:   symlinkSync(join('..', '..', '.skilled', 'commands', 'README.txt'), join(commandsDir, 'README.txt'));\n223:   writeFileSync(join(commandsDir, 'goal-opencode.md'), '# goal\\n');\n224:   writeFileSync(join(commandsDir, 'stray.md'), '# stray\\n');\n225:   const expected = [\n226:     { mirror: '.opencode/commands/deep', source: '.skilled/commands/deep' },\n227:     { mirror: '.opencode/commands/README.txt', source: '.skilled/commands/README.txt' },\n228:   ];\n229:   assert.deepEqual(findOrphans(expected, root), ['.opencode/commands/stray.md']);\n230: });\n231: \n232: test('derives each DSH adapter link from the hook library and flags a real file or a missing link', () => {\n233:   const root = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'sync-runtime-mirrors-dsh-'));\n234:   try {\n235:     const write = (relativePath, text = 'export {};\\n') => {\n236:       mkdirSync(dirname(join(root, relativePath)), { recursive: true });\n237:       writeFileSync(join(root, relativePath), text);\n238:     };\n239:     write('.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/index.mjs');\n240:     write('.skilled/hooks/overlay-guard/dsh/overlay-guard.mjs');\n241:     write('.skilled/hooks/overlay-guard/dsh/overlay-guard.test.mjs');\n242:     wri